// SignatureIndex — the WHOLE-PROGRAM DECLARATION index: every function, every ranged typealias and
// every top-level constant DECLARED in any file, read straight off the TOKENS before any body is
// parsed.
//
// WHY IT MUST EXIST, AND WHY IN THE PARSER. Three source-level decisions cannot be postponed past
// the parser, and each needs a declaration that may live in another file:
//
//   • A CALL'S RESULT TYPE. A bool `and`/`or` is CONTROL FLOW (`Parser.emitShortCircuit` — blocks
//     and a merge phi, which no later pass can retrofit); `not` picks a different OPCODE per
//     operand type; and every operand rule in TypeRules reads a value's tag. By the time a later
//     pass could supply a callee's return type, every decision that needed it has already been
//     made.
//
//   • A SHIFT'S FILL. `shr` is ARITHMETIC on a signed left operand and LOGICAL on an unsigned one
//     (TypeRules' ⭐ THE SHIFT RULE), and Maxon's unsigned type is a ranged alias whose low bound
//     is 0. The two are DIFFERENT x64 instructions, and the Std tier they lower into is TYPELESS —
//     an i64 there carries no signedness — so the opcode must be chosen while the declared type is
//     still in view, which is here. `u64.max shr 60` is 15 or -1 depending on it.
//
//   • A TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT'S VALUE. A `let` at module scope is a COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT that every
//     use INLINES as a literal (`Parser.topLevelConstantReference`), so the parser needs the NUMBER,
//     not a promise of one. It cannot fold the initializer itself: a forward reference
//     (`let TOTAL = FIRST + SECOND` above `let FIRST = 30`) and a cross-file `export let` are both
//     ordinary, and neither is in view while one file is parsed. So the fold happens HERE, after
//     every file's declarations are known — see `evaluateInitializers`.
//
// So the RANGES travel beside the return types, and the CONSTANTS beside both. An alias declared in
// `a.maxon` and used in `b.maxon` has to reach `b`'s parse, or the shift in `b` is a silent wrong
// answer — and reading the file-local alias table instead would be the identical mistake this index
// exists to fix, wearing a different hat.
//
// The FILE-LOCAL version of this — one token sweep of the file being parsed — is why the
// single-file case was right. Cross-file it was not, and the failure was a WRONG ANSWER, not a
// rejection:
//
//     a.maxon:  export function isReady() returns bool
//     b.maxon:  let x = isReady()
//               return x + 41        ⇒ shv2 COMPILED it, and returned 42
//                                    ⇒ bootstrap: E2004: Cannot operate on bool and int
//
// The parser reported an out-of-file callee as `MaxonType.unresolved`; TypeRules maps that to
// `TypeClass.unknown`; and `unknown` AGREES WITH EVERYTHING. That deferral is the right move for a
// type you genuinely cannot see, and a catastrophic one for a type you simply did not LOOK for.
// **The deferral was never the defect. Not looking was.** So this index looks — at every file's
// tokens, before any file's parse — and `unresolved` is no longer an answer for a callee some file
// declares. (Its twin was worse: agreement satisfied by deferral let `(boolean, unresolved)` reach
// `wordOpResultTag`'s bool-first arm, so `flag and crossFileInt()` MINTED a merge phi tagged
// `boolean` carrying the int 7.)
//
// THE ID PROBLEM — why a signature travels by NAME. A `MaxonType.named(id)` is meaningless outside
// the `TypeNameInterner` that minted the id, and this index is built BEFORE, and APART FROM, the
// per-file artifact interners the parser fills (ParseStaging's parallel-ready discipline: a file's
// parse touches no shared state). So the index owns its own interner, and a parser ADOPTS a
// callee's return type out of it — the type's NAME is re-interned into the parser's own artifact
// table, so the `MaxonType` handed back is indistinguishable from one that file had parsed itself.
// `returnTypeOf` is the one door, and it is the one place the two id spaces meet.

// The shape discriminators the signature hash mixes — one per arm of the two type unions, so two
// declarations that differ only in shape (`returns bool` vs `returns int`) cannot hash alike.
//
// Small distinct constants, NOT the enums' ordinals: an ordinal is not a value in Maxon, and — the
// real reason — a hash that moved when an enum's arms were REORDERED would invalidate every parse
// memo in the project for a change that means nothing.
let ReturnShapeVoid = 1 as ContentHash
let ReturnShapeValue = 2 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeBoolean = 3 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeInteger = 4 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeFloat = 5 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeNamed = 6 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeExitCode = 7 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeUnresolved = 8 as ContentHash

// A ranged typealias's declaration, mixed into the same hash as a function's. Its own
// discriminator, so a file that replaces a function with an alias of the same name moves the hash
// rather than colliding two differently-shaped declarations on it.
let DeclShapeAlias = 9 as ContentHash

// A declared struct type's shape discriminator — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin, for its reason: a name
// that stops being a `typealias` and starts being a `type` must move the hash, not collide on it.
let DeclShapeStruct = 14 as ContentHash

// A declared enum/union's shape discriminator — `DeclShapeStruct`'s twin, for its reason: a name
// that stops being a `type` and starts being an `enum` must move the hash rather than collide.
let DeclShapeEnum = 20 as ContentHash

// Whether a declared type is a `union` (vs an `enum`), mixed into the hash beside its name. Two
// distinct constants because the only thing separating a payload-free union from an enum is the
// diagnostic WORDING, and a reader that meets a non-exhaustive match must re-parse — and so print
// "union" rather than "enum" — when `enum` becomes `union`.
let EnumKindEnum = 21 as ContentHash
let EnumKindUnion = 22 as ContentHash

// One `implements <Interface>` name on an enum/union declaration (A1s wave 2), mixed per name so a
// SECOND interface moves the hash rather than being absorbed by the first. Its own discriminator for
// `DeclShapeStruct`'s reason: the list is a distinct fact from the case list beside it.
let DeclShapeEnumConformance = 55 as ContentHash

// The `MaxonType.structRef` arm's discriminator. It is DISTINCT from `TypeShapeNamed`, and that is
// load-bearing rather than tidy: `named(Point)` and `structRef(Point)` carry the same NAME and mean
// different things in every reader — one is a ranged int, the other a pointer — so a hash that
// could not tell them apart would hand a reader a memo built under the wrong one.
let TypeShapeStructRef = 15 as ContentHash

// The `MaxonType.string` arm's discriminator (P1.2 wave A). Distinct like every other shape: a
// `String` return type hashes differently from an `int` one, so a memo built under one cannot be
// served for the other.
let TypeShapeString = 23 as ContentHash

// A FUNCTION value's type shape (P1.5-A1) — so a `returns UnaryOp` return type hashes distinctly. Its
// own discriminator for `TypeShapeStructRef`'s reason. (A function type reaches the SCANNED signature
// path only if a future producer stores one; the sweep records a function-alias reference as `named`
// until the alias is known, so today this arm is exhaustiveness insurance, hashed not panicked.)
let TypeShapeFunction = 29 as ContentHash

// A TYPE-PARAMETER reference's shape (P1.6-A) — `get() returns T` hashes distinctly. Its POSITION
// is mixed after the shape (a stable identity — declaration order — never the global id), so
// `uses A, B`'s `returns A` and `returns B` hash apart. A distinct prime for `TypeShapeStructRef`'s
// reason.
let TypeShapeTypeParameter = 31 as ContentHash

// A GENERIC-INSTANCE's shape (P1.6-A) — a `returns IntBox` hashes distinctly. The BASE name and each
// ARG's shape are mixed after it (names, never ids), so `Box with Integer` and `Box with Bool` hash
// apart. A distinct prime for `TypeShapeStructRef`'s reason.
let TypeShapeGenericInstance = 37 as ContentHash

// A `Character`'s shape (P1.8 slice B) — a builtin with no name to mix, exactly like `TypeShapeString`.
// A DISTINCT prime from it, and that is the whole point: a Character and a String share a RECORD but not
// a TYPE, so `returns String` and `returns Character` must hash apart or a signature change between them
// would serve a stale parse from every other file's memo.
let TypeShapeCharacter = 41 as ContentHash

// A declared FIELD's mutability, mixed into the hash beside its name and type (P1.1a wave 2). The
// struct twin of `DeclShapeConstant`/`DeclShapeMutable`, and distinct constants rather than a reuse
// of them: those two discriminate a top-level `let` from a `var`, and colliding a field's `let` with
// a global's would let an edit that swaps one for the other land on the hash the other already
// occupied. What rides is ACCEPTANCE — `var count` → `let count` must turn every other file's
// `c.count = 5` into E2013, and a reader whose memo did not move would go on compiling it.
let FieldShapeMutable = 16 as ContentHash
let FieldShapeImmutable = 17 as ContentHash

// Whether a field declares a DEFAULT, mixed beside the payload when it does. Two constants and not
// one, because "has no default" must hash differently from "defaults to 0" — the payload alone
// cannot say so, and those two declarations accept different programs (`Self{}` is E3086 under the
// first and legal under the second).
let FieldShapeNoDefault = 18 as ContentHash
let FieldShapeDefaulted = 19 as ContentHash

// The two shapes a top-level constant's ANSWER hashes under: it evaluated to a value, or it did
// not. Their own discriminators for `DeclShapeAlias`'s reason — a name that stops being a function
// and starts being a constant must move the hash rather than collide.
let ConstShapeValue = 10 as ContentHash
let ConstShapeNoValue = 11 as ContentHash

// The THIRD shape a top-level constant's answer hashes under (P1.7a): it folded to a managed LITERAL.
// Its own discriminator for `ConstShapeValue`'s reason — a `let X = 0` that becomes `let X = "0"` must
// move the hash, and the payload alone cannot say so (the bytes `"0"` and the number 0 are different
// facts that a shared shape would let collide).
let ConstShapeManagedValue = 45 as ContentHash

// Which managed literal a constant folded to, mixed beside its bytes (P1.7a). Two constants because a
// `String` and an `Array with Byte` over the SAME bytes are different types with different records, so
// a reader that saw one must re-parse when it becomes the other.
let ManagedShapeString = 46 as ContentHash
let ManagedShapeByteArray = 47 as ContentHash

// The FOURTH shape a top-level binding's answer hashes under (P1.7 slice 2b): it folded to an ARRAY
// LITERAL. Its own discriminator for `ConstShapeManagedValue`'s reason — `let X = "ab"` becoming
// `let X = [1, 2]` turns every reader's materialized literal into a `.data` load, and nothing else on
// the hash says so.
let ConstShapeArrayValue = 48 as ContentHash

// Which ELEMENT type an array literal folded to, mixed beside its label (P1.7 slice 2b). Two constants
// for `ManagedShapeString`/`ManagedShapeByteArray`'s reason: `Array with Integer` and `Array with String`
// are different types, and a reader types its load from the instance this picks.
let ArrayElementShapeInteger = 49 as ContentHash
let ArrayElementShapeString = 50 as ContentHash

// A declaration's MUTABILITY, mixed into the hash beside its value. `let X = 5` and `var X = 5`
// are the same name and the same number and compile to COMPLETELY different code — one inlines
// as a literal, the other loads from `.data` — so a reader's parse memo must move when one
// becomes the other. Without these, changing `let` to `var` would leave every reader's memo
// valid and every reader still inlining a number that now has storage behind it.
let DeclShapeConstant = 12 as ContentHash
let DeclShapeMutable = 13 as ContentHash

// A function's PER-PARAMETER CONSUME BITS (P1.4a wave 2), mixed into the hash beside its name so a
// cross-file caller re-parses when a callee's consume set changes. What rides is ACCEPTANCE and OWNERSHIP:
// a param that becomes CONSUMED turns every caller's `f(ownedLocal)` into a move (the local is poisoned,
// E3102 on reuse) and its `f(borrowedValue)` into E2015, while the caller's own bytes never move — so a
// reader whose memo did not move would keep the old drop behaviour, a double-free or a leak. Its own
// discriminator for `DeclShapeAlias`'s reason. `ConsumeBitSet`/`ConsumeBitClear` distinguish a set bit
// from a clear one so "param 0 consumed, param 1 not" hashes differently from the reverse.
let DeclShapeParamConsume = 24 as ContentHash
let ConsumeBitSet = 25 as ContentHash
let ConsumeBitClear = 26 as ContentHash

// A function's `throws E` clause discriminator (P1.4b). Its own discriminator for `DeclShapeAlias`'s
// reason: a callee that GAINS or CHANGES a throws type changes whether each call site needs a `try`
// (E3055/E3057) and what type the `(e)` binding recovers, so a caller's parse memo must move when the
// callee's throws clause does. Only THROWING functions are recorded (absence = non-throwing default),
// exactly as `recordParamConsume` records only consuming ones — so a non-throwing function costs no
// hash movement.
let DeclShapeThrows = 27 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A declared RETURN TYPE's pre-erasure SPELLING (A2j / A2m) — the `DeclaredSurface` word `mixDeclaration`
// folds in beside the return type. It is the one declaration fact `mixReturnType` structurally cannot carry:
// `__ManagedMemory` resolves to the SAME `genericInstance` a `ByteArray` does, so an edit between the two
// spellings moves the return TYPE not at all while changing which member roster every caller's `let m = f()`
// may then reach. A DISCRIMINATOR plus the word, rather than a tag pair, because A2m made the fact a word
// and not a bit: mixing the word alone would let a plain surface (0) collide with the seed.
let ReturnShapeSurface = 56 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A declared struct FIELD's pre-erasure SPELLING (A2j / A2m) — `ReturnShapeSurface`'s twin, one
// declaration kind over, and load-bearing for the identical reason: `var buf as __ManagedMemory` and
// `var buf as ByteArray` fold identically through `mixField`'s type, while a read of that field reaches a
// different roster. Mixed by `mixField`, so it rides `recordStruct`'s one hash write like every other
// per-field fact.
let FieldShapeSurface = 58 as ContentHash

// A FUNCTION typealias's shape discriminator (P1.5-A1) — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin, for its reason: a name
// that stops being a ranged alias and becomes a function one, or whose function return type changes,
// changes what a caller binding a value of it compiles to, so a caller's parse memo must move with it.
let DeclShapeFunctionAlias = 28 as ContentHash

// A generic-instance TYPEALIAS's shape discriminator (P1.6-A) — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin. `IntBox =
// Box with Integer` changing base or args changes what `IntBox.create(…)` resolves to and type-checks
// against in ANY file, so a reader's parse memo must move with it.
let DeclShapeGenericAlias = 30 as ContentHash

// A declared generic type's TYPE-PARAMETER shape discriminator (P1.6-A). A change to `type Box uses T`'s
// param list — a rename or, load-bearingly, a change in COUNT — changes which `Box with …` instantiations
// are the right arity in any file, so a reader's parse memo must move with the count.
let DeclShapeTypeParam = 32 as ContentHash

// A declared generic type's `where`-CONSTRAINT shape discriminator (P1.7a slice 2a). A change to `type Box
// uses T where T is Digest`'s constraint list — an interface swapped, added, or dropped — changes which
// `Box with …` instantiations are LEGAL (E3017) in any file and which witness table each dispatch reads, so
// a reader's parse memo must move with it. The `DeclShapeTypeParam` twin for the constraint half.
let DeclShapeWhereConstraint = 44 as ContentHash

// A constructor parameter's TYPE-PARAMETER-FEED shape discriminators (P1.6-B2). Which type-parameter
// position a parameter feeds decides whether a caller consumes a managed argument there, so a change to a
// constructor's feed map must move a caller's parse memo — the consume twin of `ConsumeBitSet`/`Clear`.
let FeedShapeFeeds = 38 as ContentHash
let FeedShapeNone = 39 as ContentHash

// A generic type's INNER ranged typealias shape discriminator (P1.6-C) — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin, for its
// reason: a change to `type Wrapper`'s `typealias Idx` (its range, or whether it exists) changes what
// `Instance.Idx` resolves and type-checks against in ANY file, so a reader's parse memo must move with it.
let DeclShapeInnerAlias = 40 as ContentHash

// An `interface` DECLARATION's shape discriminator (R7 for its `uses` arity, R8 for its requirements) —
// `DeclShapeTypeParam`'s twin, and load-bearing for exactly its reason. The arity decides how many arguments
// an unparenthesized `implements I with A, B` binds (`Parser.interfaceUsesArity`), so adding or removing a
// `uses` name changes what a conformance in ANY file PARSES — the comma after an argument stops being another
// argument and becomes another interface. A reader that did not re-parse would go on binding the old count.
let DeclShapeInterface = 51 as ContentHash

// One interface METHOD's shape discriminator (R8) — `DeclShapeInterface`'s per-requirement twin, and its own
// so that moving a requirement between two interfaces moves the hash. A witness dispatch in any file reads
// this method's slot index, result type, formals and ABI (`Parser.findWitnessDispatchCandidates`) and bakes all of them
// into its own emitted code, so a change here has to re-parse every reader. See `recordInterfaceDeclaration`
// for which fields ride and which deliberately do not.
let DeclShapeInterfaceMethod = 52 as ContentHash

// One `extends` PARENT NAME's shape discriminator (R10c) — `DeclShapeInterfaceMethod`'s twin, and its own
// for that twin's reason: a witness dispatch numbers its slot against the interface's TRANSITIVE requirement
// list (`interfaceWitnessSlots`), so gaining, losing or REORDERING a parent moves a slot index that another
// file has already baked into its emitted code. Only the NAME is mixed — the parent's own requirements reach
// this hash through the parent's own `recordInterfaceDeclaration`, one hash being folded over every
// declaration in the program.
let DeclShapeInterfaceParent = 60 as ContentHash

// One interface requirement's RECEIVER KIND (A2w review) — a PRESENCE PAIR for `mixThrowsClause`'s reason and
// not a marker on the `static` arm alone: both kinds are possible in this position, so a hash that mixed
// nothing for the instance arm would let a requirement GAIN or LOSE `static` without any reader's memo moving.
// It rides because a PARSE reads it: `Parser.requireWitnessDispatchCandidates` REFUSES a dispatch whose
// requirements are all `static` (E3116) and `IrInterface.witnessTargetIsProtocol` refuses a `static` look-alike
// as `Equatable`/`Comparable` (E3005), so flipping the keyword in file A decides whether file B compiles at all
// while file B's own bytes stay put — this hash's stated rule, exactly.
let MethodShapeStatic = 61 as ContentHash
let MethodShapeInstance = 62 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A function's PER-PARAMETER DEFAULTS (BATCH2 slice 4), mixed into the hash beside its name for
// `DeclShapeParamConsume`'s reason and with the identical PRESENCE-PAIR shape. What rides is what a CALL
// SITE compiles to: a parameter that GAINS a default turns a caller's `f()` from E3036 into a legal call
// that emits a helper invocation, and one that LOSES it does the reverse — in both directions the caller's
// own bytes never move, so only this hash can carry it. The pair distinguishes "param 0 defaulted, param 1
// not" from the reverse, exactly as `ConsumeBitSet`/`ConsumeBitClear` do.
let DeclShapeParamDefault = 63 as ContentHash
let DefaultBitSet = 64 as ContentHash
let DefaultBitClear = 65 as ContentHash

// A `throws` clause's PRESENCE discriminators (R8) — the two arms of `ThrowsClause`, so `none` and
// `throwing(E)` cannot hash alike in a position where both are possible. `mixThrowsClause` is the one place a
// throws clause enters a content hash; before it existed the only mixer recorded THROWING clauses alone, where
// absence was itself the fact and no `none` marker was needed.
let ThrowsShapeNone = 53 as ContentHash
let ThrowsShapeThrowing = 54 as ContentHash

// The separator between a file's path and a constant's bare name in a FILE-PRIVATE constant's
// storage key. `|` is legal in neither a Maxon identifier nor a Windows path, so the composite is
// unambiguous. Ported verbatim from v1's `fileScopedTopLevelKey` (Project.maxon:189-191), which
// solves the identical problem the identical way.
let FileScopedKeySeparator = "|"

// An index into the constant ARENA (`ProgramSignatures.decls`). Wide range: declaration counts
// are unbounded.
export typealias TopLevelDeclIndex = int(0 to u64.max)
export typealias TopLevelDeclIndexArray = Array with TopLevelDeclIndex
typealias TopLevelDeclIndexMap = Map with (ByteArray, TopLevelDeclIndex)

// How many of those declarations have a `.data` slot — a SUBSET of the arena, so the same wide range.
// See `ProgramSignatures.storedGlobalCount`.
export typealias StoredGlobalCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// WHERE a top-level constant is filed, and the ONE place a constant's visibility becomes a fact
// about its NAME.
//
// A FILE-PRIVATE constant is filed under a key carrying its declaring file's path; an EXPORTED one
// under its bare name. That single distinction is what makes `file-private-same-name-cross-file`
// work — two files may each declare `let SHARED` with a different value, and each file's reads
// resolve to its OWN — and it is why "reject duplicates" is not merely unnecessary here but
// FORBIDDEN by the spec.
//
// Ported from v1's `topLevelConstStorageName` (Parser.maxon:3221-3226) + `fileScopedTopLevelKey`
// (Project.maxon:189-191). v1's rule has three conditions (a bare name, file-private, immutable);
// shv2's has ONE, and P1.0d.5b is why the third is gone.
//
// ⭐ **THE KEY EXTENDS TO A `var`. THE LABEL IS WHAT DOES NOT.** This comment used to say the
// opposite — "when the `var` rung lands it MUST NOT extend this scoping" — and it was half right
// in a way worth preserving, because the half it saw is real:
//
//   • IDENTITY must be scoped. Two files may each declare a file-private `counter`
//     (`global-file-private-same-name.md`, and `top-level-let.md` states the same rule for a
//     `let`), and each file's reads must resolve to ITS OWN. That is a property of the KEY, it is
//     the same property for both kinds of declaration, and rejecting the second declaration is not
//     an available reading of the spec.
//   • What the old comment correctly saw is that a `var` needs something a `let` never did: a
//     **LABEL**. A constant inlines at every read, so there is no slot and no name in the IR. A
//     var's slot is real, and a label carrying a FILE PATH would put a temp directory into every
//     golden fragment and into the emitted binary.
//
// It concluded that the key therefore could not be scoped. The actual conclusion is that these are
// TWO facts and want two fields: **keep ONE key, and DERIVE the label from it** (`dataLabelFor`) —
// path-free, bare-name-based, disambiguated only where two keys collide on one label.
//
// ⚠ **v1 CONCEDES THE BUG THIS AVOIDS.** Its `not mutable` condition leaves file-private vars
// sharing one bare key, and its own comment admits the reason is empirical, not structural: "In
// practice no two file-private vars in the self-hosted/stdlib tree share a bare name"
// (Parser.maxon:3206-3214). The C# bootstrap has the same defect and it is MEASURED: its resolver
// is file-scoped while its `.data` label is global-by-name, so `global-file-private-same-name.md`
// returns 212 where 118 is correct — `bumpA()` reads featB's slot. Correctness resting on a
// property of the corpus is not correctness. NONE of v1's four bare-key sites is ported.
function topLevelStorageKey(name ByteArray, visibility Visibility, declFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
	if visibility == Visibility.file 'filePrivate'
		return fileScopedDeclKey(name, declFilePath: declFilePath)
	end 'filePrivate'
	return name
end 'topLevelStorageKey'

// The key a FILE-PRIVATE declaration is filed under. Also the first probe `declFor` makes, which
// is why it is a function and not an inlined interpolation at the two sites: a reader and a writer
// that spelled this key differently would file a declaration nothing could ever find.
//
// ⚠ **IT COSTS ~4 ALLOCATIONS PER CALL** — `String.from`, the interpolation, `toByteArray` — and
// `declFor` builds one on EVERY top-level-name probe, before it knows whether the name is even
// file-private. Ranged typealiases resolve by the same scoped-first rule and deliberately do NOT
// come through here: they are asked once per named TYPE reference and would have paid this per use
// of any name two files declare (measured at 24 allocations per use — see `RangedAliasRegistry`).
// They use a two-level map instead, which needs no composite key. This site keeps the string key
// because `declIndex` is one flat map shared with `topLevelStorageKey`'s writes; giving it the same
// treatment is a change to that map's shape and to its writer, not to this function.
// EXPORTED because the P1.9 collision-suppression set is keyed the same way and for the same reason —
// "which FILE's declaration is this?" — and a second composite-key builder would be free to spell the
// separator differently from this one.
export function fileScopedDeclKey(name ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
	return "{declFilePath.toString()}{FileScopedKeySeparator}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
end 'fileScopedDeclKey'

// The prefix every top-level `var`'s `.data` label carries — a NAMESPACE, and today nothing more.
//
// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT ROUTE THE SECTION, and an earlier draft of this comment claimed it did.** What
// puts a global in writable `.data` is the RELOCATION KIND: `encodeLeaRegGlobal` (X64Backend) files
// every global's `lea` as `RdataRelocKind.dataSectionRipRelDisp32` unconditionally, and
// `patchRdataRelocationsX86` measures that kind — and only that kind — from `.data`'s RVA. The
// label's TEXT is never consulted on the way. MEASURED: with this constant set to `"zzz_"` the
// suite's globals still land in `.data`, still take their stores, and the program still returns the
// right answer. The claim it "would be classified `rdata` and fault on its first store" was false.
//
// So what it buys is READABILITY — `__data_counter` in a golden fragment says what it is — plus
// v1's convention, which the day a global is registered BY NAME will matter for real.
//
// ⭐ **THIS IS NOW THE ONLY COPY OF THIS STRING.** There was a second — a literal `"__data_"` inside
// `classifyGlobalLabel` (CodeResult.maxon) — and the two could not disagree only because that whole
// family was DEAD: zero callers, ported ahead of a use that never came. It was left in place on the
// prediction that it "plausibly becomes live when the `__slab_`/`__gt_` runtime globals arrive"; P1.0r
// then shipped `__slab_cursor` and `__slab_end` and wired nothing to it, which falsified the
// prediction, so the family was deleted rather than kept as a copy waiting to go stale. A future
// name-based classifier single-sources its prefix HERE.
let DataLabelPrefix = "__data_"

// ⭐ What separates a `.data` label from its collision-breaking ordinal — and the character is
// load-bearing, not cosmetic. **`$` is legal in no Maxon identifier**, so `__data_counter$1` is a
// label only this disambiguator can ever mint: no user-written `var` can collide with one. That is
// exactly what lets `uniqueDataLabel` COUNT instead of SEARCH (below).
let DataLabelDisambiguator = "$"

// How many `.data` labels `assignDataLabels` has minted for each BARE label so far.
//
// A COUNTER, not a set of taken labels — and the difference is a quadratic. The obvious shape is
// "probe `__data_counter`, then `$1`, then `$2`, … until one is free", which is O(k) probes for the
// k-th global of a name and therefore **Θ(n²) for n same-named file-private globals** — precisely
// the shape `global-file-private-same-name.md` is about, so precisely the shape that must not be
// the slow path. The counter answers in O(1) because it does not have to ask whether a candidate is
// free: `$` is unwritable in source (see `DataLabelDisambiguator`), so this map is the ONLY minter
// of suffixed labels and its count IS the next free ordinal. Nothing else can have taken it.
typealias DataLabelCountMap = Map with (ByteArray, MachineWord)

// `name`'s `.data` label, and the bump of `minted` that reserves it. The FIRST global of a bare name
// gets the bare label; the k-th gets `__data_<name>$<k-1>`.
//
// O(1), with no search — see `DataLabelCountMap`. Deterministic because `assignDataLabels` walks the
// arena in declaration order (source-path, then in-file), so WHICH declaration gets the bare name is
// a function of the program and not of a hash iteration order.
function uniqueDataLabel(name ByteArray, minted DataLabelCountMap) returns ByteArray
	let bare = "{DataLabelPrefix}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
	let alreadyMinted = try minted.get(bare) otherwise 0
	minted.upsert(bare, value: alreadyMinted + 1)

	if alreadyMinted == 0 'firstOfThisName'
		return bare
	end 'firstOfThisName'

	return "{String.from(bare)}{DataLabelDisambiguator}{alreadyMinted}".toByteArray()
end 'uniqueDataLabel'

// A top-level constant's state, from `pending` through to its answer. ONE union rather than a value
// table plus a side set of in-progress names, because the two questions the evaluator asks — "what
// is this worth?" and "am I already inside it?" — are the same question asked at different times,
// and a name that could be in one structure but not the other is exactly how a cycle goes undetected.
export union TopLevelConstant
	// Read by the sweep, not yet evaluated. Every decl starts here.
	pending
	// On the current evaluation's DFS stack: a reference that reaches this decl again is a CYCLE.
	evaluating
	// It evaluated. `payload` is the number (a bool's is its 0/1 — TypeRules' `BoolTrueValue`), and
	// `tag` says which of the two it is. This is what a use INLINES.
	value(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag)
	// THIS declaration is defective, and `error` is the positioned diagnostic for it. Held rather
	// than reported, because the sweep that found it is deliberately TOLERANT and has no business
	// vetoing a program (see `foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`). The REAL parse raises it, once, when it
	// reaches this line — which is also the only place that knows the diagnostic is about source the
	// user actually wrote rather than a name some other file mentioned.
	failed(error ParseError)
	// It has no value only because a constant it READS has none. Silent by design: a second
	// diagnostic here would name a line whose only fault is mentioning a broken name. v1 does the
	// same, dropping cycle victims so their readers see a plain "Undefined name" rather than a
	// cascade (TypeResolution.maxon:8332-8338).
	poisoned
	// ⭐ **IT EVALUATED TO A MANAGED LITERAL** (P1.7a top-level managed `let`) — `bytes` is the decoded
	// payload and `kind` says which literal it came from. The `value` arm's twin, and a SEPARATE arm
	// rather than a widened one because the two payloads are different things: a number a reader copies
	// into a `literal` op, versus bytes a reader re-materializes as a record.
	//
	// ⚠ **APPENDED AT THE END, on purpose.** Three range arms read this union (`value to poisoned`,
	// `failed to poisoned`, `pending to evaluating`); appending leaves every one of them EXCLUDING this
	// case, so each site fails to compile until it has been visited and decided — where inserting it
	// beside `value` would have let `value to poisoned` swallow it silently. Same rule, same reason, as
	// `StdOp`'s bands.
	managedValue(bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind)
	// ⭐ **IT EVALUATED TO AN ARRAY LITERAL** (P1.7 slice 2b) — its already-folded `elements` and the one
	// `element` kind they all share. Its own arm rather than a widened `managedValue` because an array's
	// value is neither a number nor bytes: it is a LIST, and the record built from it is a HEAP record
	// with a `.data` slot — so a binding that folds to one is STORED whichever keyword declared it (see
	// `TopLevelDecl.hasStorage`), where a `managedValue` `let` is inlined at every read.
	//
	// ⚠ APPENDED AT THE END, for `managedValue`'s reason and now with one more range arm reading this
	// union. Appending leaves `value to poisoned`, `failed to poisoned`, `pending to evaluating` and
	// `value to managedValue` all EXCLUDING this case, so each site fails to compile until it has been
	// visited and decided.
	arrayValue(elements ConstArrayElementArray, element ConstArrayElementKind)
end 'TopLevelConstant'

// ONE folded ELEMENT of a top-level array literal (P1.7 slice 2b) — what `__module_init` materializes and
// pushes. Deliberately NOT `ConstInitializerValue` (which would make it recursive): a NESTED array literal
// is not an element an array literal accepts, in a function body or here, so the type says so rather than
// leaving a case for a walk to reject.
export union ConstArrayElement
	// An integer element: the folded number, materialized as an ordinary `literal` op. The whole scalar
	// evaluator stands behind it, so `[BASE, BASE + 2]` is as much an element as `[1, 2]` is.
	integer(payload ParsedInt)
	// A String element: the literal's decoded bytes, materialized as the immortal `.rdata` record and then
	// CLONED — the array's `element_destroy@40` walk drops every live slot, so each element must be a record
	// the array may own (exactly what `Parser.moveElementIntoArray` promotes a borrowed String literal to).
	string(bytes ByteArray)
end 'ConstArrayElement'

export typealias ConstArrayElementArray = Array with ConstArrayElement

// WHICH element type a top-level array literal settled on — the ONE fact its `Array with T` instance, its
// element size and its element destructor all follow from, decided from the FIRST element and enforced on
// every later one (`ProgramSignatures` reads it and never re-inspects the elements).
//
// It mirrors `ConstArrayElement`'s arms because it IS their classification, and `constArrayElementKindOf`
// is the one function that connects the two — so the kind a global is typed by and the elements the init
// emits cannot come to disagree.
export enum ConstArrayElementKind
	integer
	string
end 'ConstArrayElementKind'

// The classification of one folded element — the single bridge between `ConstArrayElement` (what an element
// IS) and `ConstArrayElementKind` (what the ARRAY is). Both the first element's settling and every later
// element's homogeneity check read it, so a literal cannot be typed by one reading and checked by another.
export function constArrayElementKindOf(element ConstArrayElement) returns ConstArrayElementKind
	return match element 'e'
		integer gives ConstArrayElementKind.integer
		string gives ConstArrayElementKind.string
	end 'e'
end 'constArrayElementKindOf'

// WHICH managed literal a top-level `let` folded to — the ONE fact a use site needs beyond the bytes,
// because the two kinds materialize through different record shapes: a String literal is a wholly
// immortal `.rdata` record, while a byte string is an owned `Array with Byte` record over an immortal
// `.rdata` blob. Both are `LowerMaxonToStd`'s existing literal lowerings, reached through the ops the
// use site emits — this enum picks WHICH op, and nothing else.
export enum ManagedConstKind
	string
	byteArray
end 'ManagedConstKind'

// ⭐ **ONE MANAGED TOP-LEVEL BINDING — its `.data` label, the bytes its initializer folded to, and the
// type it denotes** (P1.7 slice 2a). What `managedGlobals` hands the module-init/cleanup synthesis, so the
// slot the layout reserves, the record the init builds and the drop the cleanup emits all read ONE
// description of the same global rather than three re-derivations off the arena.
//
// `range` is the initializer's first token, so every op the synthesis emits carries a span pointing at the
// declaration the user wrote — the ops exist because of that line and nothing else.
export type ManagedGlobal
	export let label as ByteArray
	// ⭐ **THE RECORD `__module_init` BUILDS, AS A DESCRIPTION** — a union rather than a `kind` beside a
	// `bytes` that only two of the three kinds have anything to put in (P1.7 slice 2b). Each arm carries
	// exactly what its own construction needs and nothing else, so an array's elements cannot be read off a
	// String global and a String's bytes cannot be read off an array's.
	export let initializer as ManagedGlobalInitializer
	export let valueType as MaxonType
	// ⭐ THE RUNTIME ENTRY `__maxon_global_cleanup` DROPS THIS SLOT'S OCCUPANT THROUGH — decided HERE, in
	// the arena walk, because the routing (`managedFieldDropCallee`) has to resolve the type's ids against
	// the SIGNATURES interner and only this side holds it. Carrying the answer is what lets the synthesis
	// take no `ProgramSignatures` at all, so there is no second place a drop callee could be derived from.
	export let dropCallee as ByteArray
	export let range as SourceRange

	export static function create(label ByteArray, initializer ManagedGlobalInitializer, valueType MaxonType, dropCallee ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ManagedGlobal
		return Self{label: label, initializer: initializer, valueType: valueType, dropCallee: dropCallee, range: range}
	end 'create'
end 'ManagedGlobal'

export typealias ManagedGlobalArray = Array with ManagedGlobal

// ⭐ **HOW ONE MANAGED GLOBAL'S INITIAL OWNED RECORD IS BUILT** — three arms because there are three record
// constructions, and each differs in whether the literal's own materialization is ALREADY the owned record
// the slot must hold. That is a fact about the record formats, not a choice `ModuleInit` makes.
export union ManagedGlobalInitializer
	// A `String` literal is a WHOLLY immortal `.rdata` record shared by every read of those bytes, so the
	// slot's occupant is a CLONE of it — the `(box) -> newBox` dual of the `__str_decref` the cleanup drops
	// it with, so the pair balances by construction.
	stringRecord(bytes ByteArray)
	// A `b"…"` literal ALREADY materializes as an owned heap 48-byte `Array` record over an immortal `.rdata`
	// blob, which is precisely what the slot must hold — so there is nothing to clone.
	byteStringRecord(bytes ByteArray)
	// An ARRAY literal (P1.7 slice 2b) has no literal record at all: it is BUILT, `__arr_create` then one
	// `__arr_push` per element. `elementSize` and `elementDestroy` are what `__arr_create` stamps into
	// `element_size@24` / `element_destroy@40`, resolved HERE (where the instance registry is) rather than
	// re-derived by the synthesis — the same discipline `dropCallee` above follows.
	arrayRecord(elements ConstArrayElementArray, elementSize ByteOffset, elementDestroy ArrayElementDestroy)
end 'ManagedGlobalInitializer'

// ⭐ **THE `element_destroy@40` STAMP AN ARRAY RECORD IS BORN WITH** — the ADDRESS of the element's drop
// callee for a MANAGED element, which `__arr_decref`'s last-owner walk calls through a `callIndirect` on
// each live slot, and NOTHING for a trivial one (an inline element owns nothing, so the walk is skipped).
//
// A union rather than a callee name whose emptiness means "trivial": the two are different instructions to
// the emitter (a `functionRef` op versus a `literal 0`), and a sentinel would let a genuinely missing name
// read as a deliberate absence.
export union ArrayElementDestroy
	trivial
	drop(callee ByteArray)
end 'ArrayElementDestroy'

// WHICH record a managed LITERAL global's bytes build — the `ManagedConstKind` → `ManagedGlobalInitializer`
// map, in one place so the kind a constant folded to and the construction its slot gets cannot drift apart.
function managedLiteralInitializer(bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind) returns ManagedGlobalInitializer
	return match kind 'k'
		string gives ManagedGlobalInitializer.stringRecord(bytes)
		byteArray gives ManagedGlobalInitializer.byteStringRecord(bytes)
	end 'k'
end 'managedLiteralInitializer'

// The value a managed global's `.data` slot holds BEFORE `__module_init` runs: a null pointer. Named
// rather than a bare 0 because it is a CLAIM — "no record exists yet" — and it is the one state the
// cleanup's unconditional decref relies on never being observed (see `ModuleInit`).
let ManagedGlobalInitialSlot = 0 as ParsedInt

// The span every op a declaration's module-init/cleanup synthesis emits carries: the declaration's
// INITIALIZER, which is the source the record is built from. `TopLevelDecl` keeps its declaring file's
// token array precisely so a reference from anywhere can be resolved without the query layer, and this
// is the same read `evalConstInitializer` seats its cursor on.
function declInitializerRange(decl TopLevelDecl) returns SourceRange
	return SourceRange.ofToken(try decl.tokens.get(decl.initStart) otherwise panic("declInitializerRange: '{String.from(decl.name)}' has no token at its recorded initializer start {decl.initStart} — the sweep records a range inside the array it carries"))
end 'declInitializerRange'

// A top-level `let` OR `var`, as the declaration sweep found it: WHAT it is called, WHETHER it is
// mutable, WHERE it was written, and the TOKEN RANGE of its initializer — read, never evaluated on
// the spot.
//
// ⭐ **ONE ARENA FOR BOTH KINDS, AND THAT IS THE RUNG'S CENTRAL CLAIM.** A top-level `var` is not a
// new declaration form; it is this one with `mutable: true` and a home. Its initializer obeys the
// IDENTICAL rule (`static-variables.md`: "Top-level `var` initializers must be constant expressions
// (same rules as `let`)"), so it wants the identical evaluator, the identical memoized DFS, the
// identical cycle detection and the identical forward-reference-by-deferred-evaluation trick. A
// second arena would be a second reading of the same grammar — the duplication this file's header
// says the compiler pays for hardest — and the two would be free to disagree about what `BASE * 2`
// is worth. What a `var` adds is settled in exactly two fields: `mutable` and `dataLabel`.
//
// **THE RANGE, AND NOT A TREE.** v1 lowers each initializer into a flat arena of structural nodes
// (`Project.unresolvedConstExprs`, Project.maxon:3361-3432) because its parse and its resolution are
// separated by whole passes: by the time TypeResolution evaluates a constant, the Parser that read it
// is long gone. shv2 has no such gap — the sweep and the evaluation are two halves of ONE query
// (`Queries.queryProgramSignatures`), and the token memo is live throughout — so the range is
// enough, and a node union would be a THIRD representation of an expression this compiler already
// has two of. The C# bootstrap makes the same call for the same reason: its `ConstantDecl` is a
// `(TokenStart, TokenEnd)` pair (2-Parser.cs:1678) and `EvaluateConstant` rewinds a cursor onto it
// (2-Parser.cs:4960-4964).
//
// `tokens` is the DECLARING file's token array, carried so that a reference FROM ANOTHER FILE can be
// resolved without reaching back into the query layer for it. It is a reference to the very array
// `queryTokens` memoized, never a copy — the token cache retains it for the whole build regardless,
// so carrying it here costs one pointer.
export type TopLevelDecl
	// The key this declaration is filed under — `topLevelStorageKey`'s answer, computed once at
	// construction so that the sweep's write and every later read cannot spell it differently.
	export var storageKey as ByteArray
	// The BARE name, kept for the diagnostics that must print what the user wrote rather than the
	// path-qualified key the compiler files it under. (v1 needs a `displayTopLevelKey` to strip that
	// prefix back off at E2012 time; carrying the bare name costs one pointer and needs no stripping.)
	export var name as ByteArray
	// `var` or `let` — WHETHER THIS NAME MAY BE WRITTEN, and nothing else. It used to double as "does it
	// have a slot?", and P1.7 slice 2b separated the two: an array `let` is immutable AND stored, so which
	// door a USE goes through is `hasStorage` below and this bit is only what the two WRITE sites ask.
	export var mutable as bool
	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS DECLARATION HAVE A `.data` SLOT?** — the ONE criterion deciding which of the two doors
	// a USE goes through (`constantValueOf` materializes a value, `globalDeclFor` loads a slot), which decls
	// the layout reserves storage for, and which ones `assignDataLabels` labels. Read from all four, so it is
	// ONE fact rather than four re-derivations that could give a binding a slot the layout never laid down.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `var` IS NOT THE WHOLE ANSWER** (P1.7 slice 2b). Every `var` is stored — that is what a `var` IS,
	// so this starts life equal to `mutable`. But so is an immutable ARRAY: its value is a heap record built
	// by `__module_init`, and no READ can re-materialize one (a `let` String's read borrows the ONE immortal
	// `.rdata` record; an array's record is mutable by nature and per-program, so a per-read copy would be a
	// different array every time). `settleArrayValue` is the ONLY writer that flips it, which is the same
	// place `outcome` becomes `arrayValue` — so the two cannot disagree, and an array binding gets a slot
	// while `let` keeps refusing the write (E2013 at an assignment, E3019 at a receiver-writing method).
	//
	// ⚠ **A FIELD RATHER THAN A FUNCTION THAT RE-READS `outcome`.** The obvious spelling is
	// `mutable or outcome is arrayValue`, asked afresh at each of the four readers. A bool written where the
	// outcome is written says the same thing once, is O(1) at every ask, and — the part that matters — makes
	// the answer available in the window where the outcome is not yet settled, which the four readers
	// straddle (see below). MEASURED: it costs no allocations and about 24 bytes per top-level declaration.
	//
	// ⚠ It is FALSE for an unevaluated decl, and that is the right answer rather than a placeholder: the
	// only reader in that window is the evaluator's own name resolution (`evalConstNamed` → `constantDeclFor`),
	// mid-DFS, and answering "inlined" merely lets the reference through to `evaluateDecl` — which settles the
	// decl and then answers `notFound` for an array exactly as it does for a managed literal. That is what
	// makes `let A = [1, 2]` / `let B = A` E2004 in BOTH declaration orders (measured against the oracle),
	// and it is what keeps the forward reference `let TOTAL = FIRST + SECOND` above `let FIRST = 30` working.
	export var hasStorage as bool
	export var declFilePath as FilePath
	export var tokens as TokenArray
	export var initStart as TokenIndex
	export var initEnd as TokenIndex
	// The NAME's position — where a diagnostic about this declaration points.
	export var nameLine as LineNumber
	export var nameColumn as ColumnNumber
	// The evaluator's answer, mutated in place as the DFS reaches and leaves this decl.
	export var outcome as TopLevelConstant
	// ⭐ THE `.data` LABEL — this var's IR IDENTITY, and EMPTY for a `let`, which has no storage.
	//
	// DERIVED FROM THE KEY, not a second spelling of it: `assignDataLabels` walks the arena in
	// declaration order and hands each mutable decl its bare name, disambiguated ONLY where an
	// earlier decl already took that label. So the common program gets `__data_counter` and the
	// pathological one gets `__data_counter` + `__data_counter$1` — and NEITHER carries a file path,
	// which is what keeps a golden fragment free of the temp directory the spec runner compiled in.
	//
	// Empty is not a sentinel here: it is the literal truth that an immutable declaration has no
	// slot, and every reader gates on `mutable` before looking (`dataLabelOf` panics rather than
	// handing back an empty label).
	export var dataLabel as ByteArray

	export static function create(name ByteArray, mutable bool, visibility Visibility, declFilePath FilePath, tokens TokenArray, initStart TokenIndex, initEnd TokenIndex, nameLine LineNumber, nameColumn ColumnNumber) returns TopLevelDecl
		return Self{
			storageKey: topLevelStorageKey(name, visibility: visibility, declFilePath: declFilePath),
			name: name,
			mutable: mutable,
			hasStorage: mutable,
			declFilePath: declFilePath,
			tokens: tokens,
			initStart: initStart,
			initEnd: initEnd,
			nameLine: nameLine,
			nameColumn: nameColumn,
			outcome: TopLevelConstant.pending,
			dataLabel: b""
		}
	end 'create'
end 'TopLevelDecl'

export typealias TopLevelDeclArray = Array with TopLevelDecl

// What the arena knows about a bare name — `CalleeReturn`'s twin, and a union for its reason: "no
// constant of this name is visible here" is an ANSWER (the name is probably a local), not a failure.
union TopLevelDeclLookup
	found(index TopLevelDeclIndex)
	notFound
end 'TopLevelDeclLookup'

// The result of the managed-opaque-`T`-`Array`-field walk (P1.7 slice 3b-ii): `present(element)` carries the
// field's SUBSTITUTED element type (the destructor the descriptor `destroyFunc@40` needs), `absent` when the
// instance holds no such field. A union for its reason — "this instance owns no managed opaque array" is an
// ANSWER (most instances do not), not a failure — and so both the reloc-need predicate and the reloc's
// destructor resolve from ONE walk. See `managedOpaqueArrayElementOf`.
union ManagedOpaqueArrayElement
	present(element MaxonType)
	absent
end 'ManagedOpaqueArrayElement'

// What a constant expression evaluates to: the PAYLOAD, and the TAG that says what the payload
// means. A bool's payload is its 0/1 (TypeRules' `BoolTrueValue`), which is why one integer field
// serves both — and why the tag is not optional: a `boolean` 1 and an `integer` 1 are the same
// number and different values.
//
// A `type` (so, one heap object per operand folded), which the per-VALUE rules in TypeRules go to
// real lengths to avoid — and the trade is the other way here. Those run on every operator of every
// expression of every function; this runs once per top-level constant DECLARATION, of which a
// program has a handful. A tagged pair is what the code wants to say, and there is no hot path to
// pay for it.
export type ConstValue
	export var payload as ParsedInt
	export var tag as ValueTypeTag

	export static function create(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ConstValue
		return Self{payload: payload, tag: tag}
	end 'create'
end 'ConstValue'

// ⭐ **WHAT ONE TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER FOLDED TO** — the evaluator's ENTRY-POINT answer, and the one
// place the scalar/managed split is made.
//
// A constant's value is either a NUMBER or a managed literal's BYTES, and those cannot share
// `ConstValue`'s scalar payload. Answering with this union at the entry point keeps every fold BELOW
// it (`evalConstBinary`, `foldConstBinOp`, `applyConstCast`, …) scalar and untouched — so a managed
// literal is never an operand of an operator that would have to reject it, and the arithmetic rules
// stay one reading of the grammar rather than two.
export union ConstInitializerValue
	scalar(value ConstValue)
	managed(bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind)
	// An ARRAY LITERAL (P1.7 slice 2b) — its folded elements and the kind they share. It is a THIRD answer
	// rather than a `managed` with a different payload for `TopLevelConstant.arrayValue`'s reason: the
	// binding it settles has STORAGE, where a `managed` `let` has none.
	arrayLiteral(elements ConstArrayElementArray, element ConstArrayElementKind)
end 'ConstInitializerValue'

// The result of asking the evaluator for a constant's value. `failed` is not an error to report — the
// diagnostic is already recorded on the decl that owns it — it is the instruction to STOP.
export union ConstEvalOutcome
	value(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag)
	failed
	notFound
end 'ConstEvalOutcome'

// Why a constant's initializer produced no value. Distinct from `ParseError` because the evaluator
// has one outcome `ParseError` cannot express: "the defect is somewhere else, and is already
// reported".
export union ConstEvalError implements Error
	// THIS initializer is defective; `error` is the positioned diagnostic for it.
	defective(error ParseError)
	// A constant this one READS has already been diagnosed. Carries no diagnostic of its own — see
	// `TopLevelConstant.poisoned`.
	dependencyFailed
end 'ConstEvalError'

// The outcome of a USE-SITE constant lookup, after evaluation has finished. Deliberately NOT
// `ConstEvalOutcome`: a use site cannot trigger an evaluation (they are all done by then) and must
// not be able to, so it is given a type with no `failed` arm to mishandle.
export union TopLevelConstantLookup
	found(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag)
	// The MANAGED twin of `found` (P1.7a top-level managed `let`): a use re-materializes the literal
	// from these bytes rather than inlining a number. It sits BESIDE `found` — unlike
	// `TopLevelConstant.managedValue`, which is appended — because the two "a constant of this name is
	// visible here" cases want to be one range arm at the caller that only asks whether it EXISTS
	// (`Parser.rejectNonGlobalAssignment`: assigning to either is E2013).
	foundManaged(bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind)
	notFound
end 'TopLevelConstantLookup'

// The outcome of a USE-SITE lookup for a top-level `var` — `TopLevelConstantLookup`'s twin, and
// deliberately a DIFFERENT type carrying DIFFERENT payload. A constant's use needs its VALUE (it
// inlines a number); a var's use needs its LABEL and TYPE (it emits a load against a slot). One
// union carrying both would have an arm every caller had to prove it was not holding.
//
// ⚠ **THE WHOLE `MaxonType`, NOT A BARE TAG** (P1.7 slice 2a). A `var Buffer = b"…"` loads a value of the
// CONCRETE `Array with Byte` instance, and an instance is its gid — a tag alone cannot say which one, and
// a use site that re-derived it would be a second answer to "what type is this global?" free to name a
// different instance than the slot was laid out for.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE MUTABILITY, because a STORED binding is no longer necessarily a `var`** (P1.7 slice 2b). An
// array `let` has a slot — its value is a heap record no read can re-materialize — so "which slot does this
// name denote?" and "may it be written?" came apart, and this door answers the first. The second is asked by
// the two sites that WRITE (`Parser.parseTopLevelAssignment`, and the synthetic receiver binding a method
// call is dispatched on), off this bit; without it an array `let` would silently accept both.
export union TopLevelGlobalLookup
	found(label ByteArray, valueType MaxonType, mutable bool)
	notFound
end 'TopLevelGlobalLookup'

// Which kind of top-level declaration a lookup is asking for. The two are resolved by ONE probe
// (`declFor`) and are never interchangeable at a use site: an inlined binding materializes its value, a
// stored one loads from a slot.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS `inlined` vs `stored`, NOT `let` vs `var`** (P1.7 slice 2b). Those two questions were the same
// question until an array literal reached the top level: an array binding's value is a HEAP RECORD, which
// no read can re-materialize, so it gets a `.data` slot whichever keyword declared it — and a `let` differs
// from a `var` only in refusing the WRITE. `TopLevelDecl.hasStorage` is the one criterion, and naming this enum
// after the storage rather than the keyword is what stops a reader assuming they still coincide.
//
// An enum, not the bare `mutable bool` the decl carries: at a CALL SITE `wanted: DeclKind.inlined` says
// what is being asked for, where `wanted: false` would be a magic value the reader has to decode against
// the field's polarity.
enum DeclKind
	inlined
	stored
end 'DeclKind'

// The per-argument classification the three "does any interned instance of `baseName` classify its type
// parameter at `position` as …?" instance-scan gates in `ProgramSignatures` differ on — the ONE thing that
// varies between them, named here so the registry SCAN (match base name, bounds-guard the arg) lives in one
// place (`anyInstanceTypeArgHasKind`) and a fix to the loop or the bound cannot reach one gate and silently
// miss the other two.
enum TypeArgInstantiationKind
	// The box MOVES the argument in and the caller may not reuse it (`typeArgIsOwned`): a String, an owning
	// struct, a boxed union, a managed generic instance — the managed-field DROP and the reassign's
	// descriptor-gated old-value drop.
	consumeMoved
	// The box CO-OWNS a BORROWED trivial struct/instance (`typeIsManaged and not typeArgIsOwned`): the
	// retain-escaping rung's `__mm_incref`-at-feed + destructor-decref pair, which a shared-body reassign
	// cannot re-balance and therefore rejects.
	coOwnedTrivial
	// Not deep-cloneable through the single-`copyFunc` element mechanism (`not typeSupportsDeepClone(as
	// element)`): a managed-element array or a non-Array generic instance — the opaque-copy front-end reject.
	uncopyableElement
end 'TypeArgInstantiationKind'

// The `StdType` a global of `tag` occupies its `.data` slot as — which is to say WHICH TAGS MAY BE
// A GLOBAL AT ALL, because the width itself is not this function's to know.
//
// ⚠ **IT NO LONGER CARRIES ITS OWN TABLE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** It used to answer `integer`→i64
// and `boolean`→i1 in its own two arms, which made it a SECOND copy of the tag→`StdType` collapse
// that `LowerMaxonToStd.valueTagToStdType` already is — one fact written down twice, in the
// project's signature failure mode. The two copies had already begun to drift in the only way they
// could: this one had no `float` arm, so `var pi = 3.14` panicked here while the lowering was
// perfectly willing to give it an f64. A global's width and a value's width are THE SAME QUESTION,
// and `data-section-f64-8byte` pins the 8 bytes that answer proves.
//
// What is genuinely local is the REACHABILITY guard below — what a top-level `var` can be typed —
// and that is a different question from how wide the type is. `named` needs an `as` cast (P1.9), so
// it panics rather than defaulting to a width.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS ALSO THE ONE PLACE THAT SAYS WHICH GLOBALS HAVE STORAGE AT ALL** (P1.7 slice 2a). It used
// to share that job with `Parser.requireStorableGlobal`, which refused a MANAGED `var` with a positioned
// E2015 while this function panicked on the same two tags — the same enumeration written down twice, and
// the pair had already begun to drift (this one had no `float` arm while the other admitted one). The
// managed refusal is gone: a managed global's slot is an 8-byte POINTER filled before `main` by
// `__module_init` (see `ModuleInit`), so `string` and `genericInstance` are ordinary reachable widths
// here, and the remaining arms are invariant backstops with no user-reachable path to them.
//
// `boolean` is ONE byte (`i1`, whose `storageBytes` is 1), which is what
// `static-variables.md`'s `data-section-bool-1byte` pins; `integer` is eight (`i64`); `float` is
// eight (`f64`); a managed pointer is eight. All of them come from the one table.
function globalStdType(tag ValueTypeTag, name ByteArray) returns StdType
	match tag 'reachable'
		boolean to float then break 'reachable'
		named then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a global's type is inferred from a bare constant initializer, so it is never a ranged alias (that needs an `as` cast, P1.9)")
		exitCode to void then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — the constant evaluator folds integers, bools and floats only")
		// A STRUCT GLOBAL is the case this guard's header was written FOR, arriving one rung early.
		// It is unreachable by the same route the others are — the constant evaluator folds no
		// `T.create(…)`, because a call in a top-level initializer is E2045 — so a struct's tag
		// cannot get this far. Were it to, the honest answer is still not a width: a struct global
		// needs a `.data` slot holding a POINTER to a box allocated before `main`, which is module
		// initialization, and shv2 has none.
		structRef then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a struct global would need its box allocated before `main` runs, which needs module initialization (not built); a call in a top-level initializer is rejected as E2045 long before this")
		// ⭐ A MANAGED global (P1.7 slice 2a) — a `String` or a `b"…"` byte string. Its slot holds a
		// POINTER to the record `__module_init` builds before `main`, so the `.data` image reserves one
		// machine word of zero and the width comes from the same table every other pointer's does. It is
		// the ONE reachable case whose slot is not its value.
		string then break 'reachable'
		// A FUNCTION global is unreachable (P1.5-A1): a global takes no type annotation and a function
		// reference is not a constant initializer, so a global's inferred type is never `function` (a bare
		// function name at top level is rejected before this — `var g = double` is E2045, no call in a
		// constant initializer). Were it to reach here, the honest answer is still not a width.
		function then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a function value is not a constant initializer, so a global's inferred type is never a function")
		// A TYPE-PARAMETER / GENERIC-INSTANCE global is unreachable (P1.6-A): a type parameter names no
		// value outside a generic body and a generic instance is a heap pointer, and a global's type is a
		// folded scalar constant — neither can be one.
		typeParameter then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a type parameter names no value at module scope")
		// A `b"…"` byte-string global: the `Array with Byte` instance whose record `__module_init` builds.
		// Reachable for the `string` arm's reason, and only through a managed LITERAL — the constant
		// evaluator folds no `Set.create()`/`Map` initializer, so no other instance can get here.
		genericInstance then break 'reachable'
		// A CHARACTER global is unreachable: a top-level binding's initializer is a folded constant, and a
		// multi-byte character literal folds to a managed `TopLevelConstant.managedValue` only for the two
		// TEXT kinds the evaluator knows (a String literal and a `b"…"` byte string). Were a Character
		// global ever admitted, its slot would be a record POINTER — `string`'s answer, not a width — so
		// this panics rather than silently sizing one.
		character then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a character literal is not a foldable constant initializer, so a global's inferred type is never a Character")
	end 'reachable'

	return valueTagToStdType(tag)
end 'globalStdType'

// What the index knows about a callee — the TWO outcomes the old `MaxonType.unresolved` sentinel
// collapsed into one, plus the third (`void`) that it dropped on the floor.
export union CalleeReturn
	// Some file declares this function, and `returnType` is its declaration UNDISTORTED: `void`
	// means it returns nothing at all (using the call as a value is an error — `Parser.parseCall`),
	// `value(t)` means it returns a `t`. The old code mapped BOTH of these and the case below onto
	// one `unresolved`, which is how `let x = noop()` then `x + 4` compiled.
	declared(returnType MaxonReturnType)
	// NO file declares it. The parser DEFERS — it types the call `unresolved` — rather than
	// reporting, and that is deliberate: the authoritative answer belongs to
	// `SemanticCheck.validateCall`, which reads the registry the REAL parse built (E3004, "call to
	// undefined function"). This token sweep is the TOLERANT half of the pair (it records only the
	// exact `function NAME ( … ) returns TYPE` shape and skips anything else without complaint), so
	// it must never gain a veto: a sweep that missed a declaration the parser accepts would reject
	// a correct program. Two whole-program tables that could each veto a call would also be one
	// fact written down twice — and this one is not the authority.
	undeclared
end 'CalleeReturn'

// What the index knows about a NAMED type — `CalleeReturn`'s twin, and a union for the same
// reason: "no file declares this" is an ANSWER, not a failure, and spelling it as a sentinel range
// (`low: 0`) would make an undeclared name read as UNSIGNED, which is the one thing it must never
// silently do.
export union DeclaredAlias
	declared(alias RangedTypeAlias)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredAlias'

// What the index knows about a FUNCTION typealias (P1.5-A1) — `DeclaredAlias`'s twin, and a union for
// the identical reason: "no file declares a function alias of this name" is an ANSWER, not a failure.
// THE door `Parser.parseTypeReference` asks "is this name a function type?" through — a hit re-tags the
// reference `MaxonType.function`; a miss leaves it a `named` for the ranged/struct/enum registries.
export union DeclaredFunctionAlias
	declared(alias FunctionTypeAlias)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredFunctionAlias'

// What the index knows about a declared STRUCT TYPE — `DeclaredAlias`'s twin, and a union for the
// identical reason. "No file declares a `type` of this name" is an ANSWER: the name is probably a
// ranged alias, or `ExitCode`, or a typo — and each of those is somebody else's question. Handing
// back an empty layout instead would make an undeclared name read as a struct with NO FIELDS, so
// `p.x` would report "no field 'x'" for a type that is not a struct at all.
export union DeclaredStruct
	declared(layout StructLayout)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredStruct'

// What the index knows about a declared ENUM/UNION type — `DeclaredStruct`'s twin, and a union for
// the identical reason. "No file declares an `enum`/`union` of this name" is an ANSWER: the name is
// probably a struct, a ranged alias, or a typo — each somebody else's question. THE door the parser
// asks every enum question through: is this scrutinee an enum? what are its cases and tags?
export union DeclaredEnum
	declared(layout EnumLayout)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredEnum'

// ⭐ **HOW A UNION PAYLOAD FIELD IS STORED AND DROPPED — P1.3 slice 2.** The ONE classifier the
// construct (move-in), the match extract (move-out), the drop routing, and the synthesized
// `__destruct_<U>` cascade all read, so "is field F managed, and if so String or struct?" is decided in
// exactly ONE place. That is the whole defence against this rung's signature bug: a field moved IN but
// not dropped leaks; dropped but not moved in double-frees — and neither can happen when one function
// decides both. It is v1's `unionHasManagedPayload` predicate generalised to also name the KIND.
export union PayloadClass
	// int / bool / exitCode / a ranged-int alias / a payload-free enum (whose value IS its i64 tag).
	// Stored inline; owns nothing; the cascade skips it.
	scalar(storage MaxonType)
	// A String (`isString`) or a struct box — an owned heap pointer. Construct MOVES it in (no incref);
	// a match binding MOVES it out; the cascade drops it (String via `__str_decref`, struct via
	// `__mm_decref`). `storage` is the field's own type, which `maxonTypeToStdType` gives a machine word.
	managed(storage MaxonType, isString bool)
	// A `float` payload — needs the `bitcastI64ToF64` INVERSE a later rung adds (a scalar-only union is
	// int-tagged, so a float payload must go in as bits and come back out as a float; only the outbound
	// half, `bitcastF64ToI64`, exists — it shipped with `float.hash`).
	floatPayload
	// A boxed-union field/payload (a nested payload-bearing union) — a MANAGED owned heap pointer, exactly
	// like a `managed` struct box: construct MOVES it in (no incref), and the cascade drops it through its
	// OWN `__destruct_<U>` (or the trivial `__mm_decref` for a scalar-only union box). It is kept a case
	// DISTINCT from `managed` so the UNION-payload path — whose store width comes from `payloadStorageOf`
	// and CANNOT carry the `named` tag through `maxonTypeToStdType` — can still reject a nested boxed-union
	// PAYLOAD cleanly (`requirePayloadLowerable`); the STRUCT-field path derives its store width from
	// `fieldStorageType` instead, so it lowers a boxed-union field fine (P1.4b wave 2c). `storage` is the
	// field's `named(<union>)` type — the value tag a struct-field type-check compares, a moved-out load
	// carries, AND the union-payload reject derives its "union U" message text from (at the error site, not
	// eagerly — the message is a cold path, so nothing precomputes a `String` on every classification).
	nestedUnion(storage MaxonType)
	// A name no file declares — `TypeResolution` reports it as E3011; the caller keeps the op
	// well-formed for the instant before the pipeline's error gate throws.
	undeclaredName
end 'PayloadClass'

// Classify a union payload field's storage and ownership. `typeNames` resolves a `named` field's id to
// a NAME; pass the interner the LAYOUT's ids live in — the file-local artifact interner at the
// construct/extract sites, `project.typeNames` at the destructor-synthesis site. All three lookups
// below are keyed by that name, so they are interner-independent once the name is in hand.
export function classifyUnionPayload(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, fieldType MaxonType) returns PayloadClass
	match fieldType 'kind'
		boolean then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		integer then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		exitCode then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		float then return PayloadClass.floatPayload
		// A String / struct reference is a managed heap pointer whose move-in and drop are this rung's
		// work. A payload field of a struct's own name resolves to `structRef` (`Self` and the enclosing
		// type); a reference to ANOTHER struct arrives as `named` and is resolved below.
		string then return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: true)
		structRef then return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: false)
		unresolved then panic("classifyUnionPayload: a payload field is declared `unresolved` — `parseTypeReference` produces only `named`, `structRef` and the three primitive keywords, so no payload declaration can carry one")
		// A FUNCTION-typed payload (P1.5-A1) is a SCALAR: a code pointer is an i64 stored inline, owns no
		// heap, and the cascade skips it (nothing to drop). It stores through `maxonTypeToStdType(function)`
		// → i64, exactly as a scalar does.
		function then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		// ⭐ A TYPE-PARAMETER field is a TRIVIAL SCALAR (P1.6-A) — and this is what keeps the shared
		// destructor sound. `T` is opaque and its trivial argument is stored as-is (an 8-byte word), so
		// the box neither increfs it on the way in (`moveInPayload` plain-stores it) nor drops it (the
		// cascade skips it — `structFieldIsManaged` is false), which makes `type Box uses T`'s destructor
		// the TRIVIAL `__mm_decref`, byte-identical to the equivalent monomorphic struct. Its concrete
		// managed argument is REJECTED at instantiation (`checkGenericInstanceArg`), so the borrow this
		// implies (the box holds a pointer it does not own) is only ever a trivial scalar or a caller-owned
		// trivial struct — never a String or a managed-field struct that would leak here.
		typeParameter then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		// A generic-INSTANCE payload is an OWNED base-struct box (P1.6-B2), exactly like a `structRef`
		// payload: construct MOVES it in, a match binds it out, the cascade drops it through its own
		// `__destruct_<instance>` / `__mm_decref` (`managedFieldDropCallee` routes the `genericInstance`
		// tag). Its storage is the `genericInstance` type itself, which `maxonTypeToStdType` gives an i64
		// (a pointer), so `payloadStorageOf`/`payloadValueTypeOf` lower it like any managed pointer.
		genericInstance then return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: false)
		// ⭐ A CHARACTER payload is an OWNED byte record — the same record a `string` payload is, so it is
		// managed and `isString: true`: `isString` selects the fused-record COPY/DROP protocol
		// (`promoteToOwnedString` in, `__str_decref` out), which is a question about the REPRESENTATION and
		// not about the type. A Character answering `false` here would take the aggregate path and try to
		// co-own a record that has no `__mm_retain` owner.
		character then return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: true)
		named then break 'kind'
	end 'kind'

	// Resolved through `denotedAggregateName` for the reason that door states: a payload spelled with a TUPLE
	// typealias (`some(v Pair)`) reaches here as a bare alias name whenever the sweep read the declaration
	// before the alias's own file was walked, and `Pair` is a registered nothing — the struct arm below missed
	// it and the binding stayed `named`, which TypeResolution then rejected as `E3011 Unknown type 'Pair'` on a
	// program the other walk order compiled and ran.
	let name = signatures.denotedAggregateName(try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) otherwise panic("classifyUnionPayload: a payload field's named type id is not in the interner it was resolved against — pass the interner the layout's ids live in"))

	// A ranged-int alias is a scalar of its underlying width; a float-underlying alias would be a float
	// payload (none are declarable yet).
	//
	// ⚠ Bare-name, and NOT the caller's file: the alias that governs a payload field is the one visible
	// where the UNION was declared, which `EnumLayout` does not record. Passing the classifying file
	// would not be a partial answer, it would be the wrong one — this is asked from the emitted
	// runtime's walk of `project.enumTypes` as well as from the parser.
	if let underlying = try signatures.aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile(name) 'alias'
		return aliasPayloadClass(underlying)
	end 'alias'

	// A payload-FREE enum's value is an i64 tag — a scalar; a BOXED union is a managed heap pointer
	// (its box carries the field's `named(<union>)` id, which `enumPayloadClass` reads off `fieldType`).
	match signatures.enumOf(name) 'asEnum'
		declared(enumLayout) then return enumPayloadClass(enumLayout, fieldType: fieldType)
		undeclared then break 'asEnum'
	end 'asEnum'

	// A `type` (struct) payload is a managed heap pointer. Its storage type is a `structRef` — NOT the
	// `named` the field was spelled with: `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on a `named` (an op's type must be
	// concrete by lowering), and a struct BINDING's field access (`b.mass`) requires the `structRef`
	// tag (`requireStructBase`). An undeclared name is a user error TypeResolution reports as E3011.
	// The id is re-interned from the RESOLVED name rather than reused off `fieldType`: for a struct spelled by
	// its own name the two are the same id (the name is already in this table), and for a tuple alias they are
	// not — the alias's id spells `Pair`, which names no struct for `requireStructBase` to resolve.
	match signatures.structOf(name) 'asStruct'
		declared then return PayloadClass.managed(MaxonType.structRef(typeNames.intern(name)), isString: false)
		undeclared then return PayloadClass.undeclaredName
	end 'asStruct'
end 'classifyUnionPayload'

// A ranged-int alias payload is a scalar of its underlying width. A float-underlying alias would be a
// float payload, rejected the same way a bare `float` is.
function aliasPayloadClass(underlying MaxonType) returns PayloadClass
	if tagIsFloat(maxonTypeTag(underlying)) 'floatAlias'
		return PayloadClass.floatPayload
	end 'floatAlias'
	return PayloadClass.scalar(underlying)
end 'aliasPayloadClass'

// A payload whose type is another enum/union: a payload-FREE enum is a scalar (its value is its i64
// tag, stored inline); a BOXED union is a MANAGED heap pointer carrying the field's own `named(<union>)`
// type (`fieldType`) — the value tag its move-in/move-out/drop all read.
function enumPayloadClass(enumLayout EnumLayout, fieldType MaxonType) returns PayloadClass
	if enumLayout.isBoxed() 'boxed'
		return PayloadClass.nestedUnion(fieldType)
	end 'boxed'
	return PayloadClass.scalar(MaxonType.integer)
end 'enumPayloadClass'

// ⭐ **THE ONE LOOP THREE QUESTIONS SHARE.** Does any case carry a payload that needs a DROP — a managed
// (String/struct) field, or (when `wantStringOnly`) specifically a String field? Keeping it ONE function,
// not one copy per site, is what makes the destructor's dropped set, the construct's moved set, and the
// `usesStrDecref` gate agree on what "managed" means by construction. Callers:
//   • `wantStringOnly: false` — does the union need a `__destruct_<U>` at all / does the construct move
//     its payloads in (`decrefCalleeFor`, `installUnionDestructors`, `noteUnionDestructorUsage`);
//   • `wantStringOnly: true`  — does that destructor transitively call `__str_decref` (the usesStrDecref
//     gate in `noteUnionDestructorUsage`).
export function unionHasManagedField(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, layout EnumLayout, wantStringOnly bool) returns bool
	for ci in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
		if caseHasManagedField(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, layout: layout, ci: ci, wantStringOnly: wantStringOnly) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachCase'
	return false
end 'unionHasManagedField'

// The per-CASE half — the destructor's tag switch emits a drop block only for a case this answers true
// for, so the switch and the union-wide gate above read the exact same classification.
export function caseHasManagedField(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, layout EnumLayout, ci CaseIndex, wantStringOnly bool) returns bool
	let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(ci)
	for fi in 0 upto payloads.count() 'eachField'
		let field = try payloads.get(fi) otherwise panic("caseHasManagedField: payloadsAt count disagrees with get at {fi}")
		let class = classifyUnionPayload(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, fieldType: field.declaredType)
		let needsDrop = payloadClassIsString(class) if wantStringOnly else payloadClassIsManaged(class)
		if needsDrop 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachField'
	return false
end 'caseHasManagedField'

// Is a classified payload a managed (String/struct) one — the single reader of "does this class own a
// heap drop?", so every site testing it (the predicate above, the destructor synthesis, the usage scan)
// agrees.
export function payloadClassIsManaged(class PayloadClass) returns bool
	return match class 'c'
		managed gives true
		nestedUnion gives true
		scalar gives false
		floatPayload gives false
		undeclaredName gives false
	end 'c'
end 'payloadClassIsManaged'

// Is a classified payload a STRING (drops through `__str_decref`) rather than a struct (`__mm_decref`)?
// The single reader of "which managed drop?", so the destructor synthesis and the usesStrDecref gate route
// the same way.
export function payloadClassIsString(class PayloadClass) returns bool
	return match class 'c'
		managed(_, isString) gives isString
		scalar gives false
		floatPayload gives false
		nestedUnion gives false
		undeclaredName gives false
	end 'c'
end 'payloadClassIsString'

// The CONCRETE storage type a classified payload occupies its slot as — what the `loadIndirect`/
// `storeIndirect` carries. A managed payload's storage is a `structRef`/`string` (an i64 pointer); a
// boxed-union (`nestedUnion`) payload's is the field's `named(<union>)` — the value tag a struct-field
// type-check compares against the value (`requireManagedValueMatches`, tag-only, never lowered here).
// A `float` payload is rejected before any store reaches here. ⚠ A `named` storage does NOT lower
// through `maxonTypeToStdType` (it panics), so the UNION-payload store — whose width comes from
// `payloadStorageOf` — must never receive a `nestedUnion` (`requirePayloadLowerable` rejects it first);
// the STRUCT-field store derives its width from `fieldStorageType` (→ i64), not from here.
export function payloadStorageOf(class PayloadClass) returns MaxonType
	match class 'c'
		scalar(storage) then return storage
		managed(storage, _) then return storage
		nestedUnion(storage) then return storage
		undeclaredName then return MaxonType.integer
		floatPayload then panic("payloadStorageOf: a float payload has no lowerable storage — requirePayloadLowerable rejects it before any store")
	end 'c'
end 'payloadStorageOf'

// The MaxonType a LOADED payload value is tagged with: a managed payload's concrete storage (so a
// struct binding reads as `structRef` — required by `requireStructBase` — and a String as `string`); a
// boxed-union payload's `named(<union>)` (so a moved-out load is recognized as a boxed union by
// `enumMatchFor`/`decrefCalleeFor`); a scalar's DECLARED type (so a ranged alias's low bound stays
// readable by the shift rule, exactly as `emitFieldLoad` keeps a scalar field's alias).
export function payloadValueTypeOf(class PayloadClass, declared MaxonType) returns MaxonType
	match class 'c'
		scalar then return declared
		managed(storage, _) then return storage
		nestedUnion(storage) then return storage
		undeclaredName then return declared
		floatPayload then panic("payloadValueTypeOf: a float payload has no lowerable value type — requirePayloadLowerable rejects it before any load")
	end 'c'
end 'payloadValueTypeOf'

// ⭐ **A SOURCE-LEVEL TYPE'S NAME for a function-type display (P1.5 #81).** A declared alias/struct/union by
// its interned NAME (so a `Integer` parameter prints `Integer`, matching the reference oracle — NOT `int`,
// which `typeTagName(named)` would give), and a bare primitive by its keyword. Reads the type's id out of
// `typeNames` — pass the interner the type's ids live in (`project.typeNames` at the whole-program merge
// site). Fed PRE-RESOLUTION types (a ranged alias is still `named(Integer)`), which is exactly why the check
// that uses it drains before `resolveTypes` erases those names to `integer`.
// ⚠ A `function`-tagged type is rendered by its ALIAS NAME for the same reason a `named` one is, and it is
// not cosmetic: `typeTagName(function)` is the bare word `function`, so a function type nested inside another
// printed as `fn(function) returns int` on BOTH sides of a mismatch — a diagnostic saying a type does not
// match itself. Its id names the alias exactly as a `named` type's does.
export function maxonTypeName(t MaxonType, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns String
	let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.named or tag == ValueTypeTag.structRef or tag == ValueTypeTag.function 'namedType'
		return String.from(try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(t)) otherwise panic("maxonTypeName: type-name id {typeNameIdOf(t)} is not in the interner — pass the interner the type's ids live in"))
	end 'namedType'
	return typeTagName(tag)
end 'maxonTypeName'

// Render a function type as `fn(P1, P2, …) returns R` from its parameter and return types (P1.5 #81). A void
// return carries no `returns` clause. The canonical signature STRING the merge check compares by name — the
// reference oracle's `MergeTernaryArmFnTypes` model, where two functions agree iff this string is equal.
export function formatFnTypeDisplay(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, returnType MaxonReturnType, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns String
	var rendered = "fn("
	for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
		if i > 0 'separator'
			rendered = "{rendered}, "
		end 'separator'
		rendered = "{rendered}{maxonTypeName(try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("formatFnTypeDisplay: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}"), typeNames: typeNames)}"
	end 'eachParam'
	rendered = "{rendered})"

	match returnType 'return'
		value(returnValue) then rendered = "{rendered} returns {maxonTypeName(returnValue, typeNames: typeNames)}"
		void then break 'return'
	end 'return'
	return rendered
end 'formatFnTypeDisplay'

export typealias PayloadClassArray = Array with PayloadClass

export typealias TypeParamIdArray = Array with TypeParamId

// ⭐ **A CONSTRUCTOR PARAMETER'S TYPE-PARAMETER FEED (P1.6-B2)** — for a parameter MOVED into a
// type-PARAMETER field (`Box.create(v T)` → `Self{value: v}`, `v` feeds `T` at position 0), the type
// parameter POSITION it fills; `none` for every other parameter (a scalar, a direct-field store, an
// unstored one). The shared body cannot decide whether `T` is managed (it is opaque there), so this
// carries the mapping OUT to the CONCRETE call site, which knows the instance's argument at that position
// and consumes the argument iff it OWNS managed heap (`genericInstanceArgIsOwned`). Trivial arguments
// (`PointBox`/`IntBox`) feed too, but the call site borrows them — the feed is inert unless the argument
// owns heap, so `generic-trivial-struct-arg` is byte-identical.
export union TypeParamFeed
	feeds(position TypeParamId)
	none

	// Does this carry a feed position — the `feeds(pos)` case rather than `none`? The boolean the transitive
	// array-feed fixpoint (P1.7 slice 3b-iv) seeds its reverse-reachability BFS from, without re-spelling the
	// `match` at every node.
	export function isFeed() returns bool
		return match self 'f'
			feeds gives true
			none gives false
		end 'f'
	end 'isFeed'
end 'TypeParamFeed'

export typealias TypeParamFeedArray = Array with TypeParamFeed

// ⭐ **A FUNCTION'S PER-PARAMETER CONSUME FACTS (P1.4a wave 2, P1.6-B2)** — the ordered `paramNames` (so a
// call site can slot a LABELLED argument `inner: i` to its position without the `FuncSignature`, which is
// not yet built at parse time), the `bits` (`bits[i]` true iff parameter `i` is CONSUMED — moved into a
// NON-type-parameter struct field, so the callee owns it whatever the concrete type), and the `feeds`
// (`feeds[i]` names the type-parameter position parameter `i` fills, for the concrete-site managed consume).
// The sweep computes them from tokens (the only whole-program input a pure per-file parse may read), one
// entry per function that consumes OR feeds, and travels by NAME with no interner (names, bools, positions —
// no ids), exactly like `enumTypes`. `feeds` is EMPTY for a non-generic function (the common case).
export type ParamConsumeInfo
	export let paramNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let bits as BoolArray
	export let feeds as TypeParamFeedArray

	export static function create(paramNames ByteArrayArray, bits BoolArray, feeds TypeParamFeedArray) returns ParamConsumeInfo
		return Self{paramNames: paramNames, bits: bits, feeds: feeds}
	end 'create'

	export function isConsumed(index ParamCount) returns bool
		if index >= self.bits.count() 'past'
			return false
		end 'past'
		return try self.bits.get(index) otherwise panic("ParamConsumeInfo.isConsumed: bits.get OOB at {index} despite index < count")
	end 'isConsumed'

	// Does ANY parameter feed a type parameter (P1.7 slice 3b-v)? The whole-`feeds`-column twin of
	// `typeParamFeedOf`'s single-index read — the descriptor-need seed asks it per method (via
	// `ProgramSignatures.functionFeedsTypeParam`). Empty for a non-generic function, so it answers false.
	export function feedsAnyTypeParam() returns bool
		for i in 0 upto self.feeds.count() 'each'
			if (try self.feeds.get(i) otherwise panic("feedsAnyTypeParam: feeds.get OOB at {i} despite i < count")).isFeed() 'feed'
				return true
			end 'feed'
		end 'each'
		return false
	end 'feedsAnyTypeParam'

	// The type-parameter position parameter `index` feeds, or `none` — the concrete-site managed-consume
	// map. Out of range (a non-generic function's empty `feeds`, an unknown label) answers `none`, exactly
	// as `isConsumed` answers `false` for its short `bits`.
	export function typeParamFeedOf(index ParamCount) returns TypeParamFeed
		if index >= self.feeds.count() 'past'
			return TypeParamFeed.none
		end 'past'
		return try self.feeds.get(index) otherwise panic("ParamConsumeInfo.typeParamFeedOf: feeds.get OOB at {index} despite index < count")
	end 'typeParamFeedOf'

	// Does this function TAKE A RECEIVER — is it an instance method rather than a static/top-level function?
	// The sweep prepends `SelfParamName` at slot 0 for an instance method's CALL-ALIGNED parameter list
	// (`callAlignedInstanceParams`) and never for a receiverless function, so the first name IS the whole test.
	// The distinction the escaping-borrow store reads: a type-parameter FEED in a STATIC constructor is a
	// `Self{item: item}` store the box CO-OWNS (retained), while a feed in an INSTANCE method is a
	// callee-owns-and-moves store into durable storage (`self.items.push(item)`, `self.saved = next` — the
	// slice-3b mechanism) whose lifetime is handled by the callee, never by a caller-side retain.
	export function isInstanceMethod() returns bool
		return self.paramNames.count() > 0 and (try self.paramNames.get(0) otherwise panic("ParamConsumeInfo.isInstanceMethod: paramNames.get(0) on a non-empty list")).equals(SelfParamName)
	end 'isInstanceMethod'
end 'ParamConsumeInfo'

export type ParamConsumeEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let info as ParamConsumeInfo

	export static function create(name ByteArray, info ParamConsumeInfo) returns ParamConsumeEntry
		return Self{name: name, info: info}
	end 'create'
end 'ParamConsumeEntry'

export typealias ParamConsumeEntryArray = Array with ParamConsumeEntry
export typealias ParamConsumeMap = Map with (ByteArray, ParamConsumeInfo)

// ⭐⭐ **A DEFAULT VALUE IS A SYNTHESIZED NULLARY FUNCTION, AND THIS IS ITS NAME (BATCH2 slice 4).**
// `function greet(name String = "World")` declares a second function, `__paramDefault#greet#0`, whose whole
// body is `return "World"`; a call site that omits the argument emits an ordinary call to it and passes the
// result. That is the ENTIRE mechanism, and everything it buys follows from "it is an ordinary call":
//
//   • **Ownership needs no new rule.** shv2 decides drops in the PARSER — a value materialized in a later
//     tier has no scope to be dropped from — so the default's value has to be born at the call site, and a
//     call RESULT is a shape the parser already owns and drops (`mintOwnedCallResult`). v1 materializes at
//     LOWERING instead, and pays for it with an explicit `classifyValue(callReturnRc1)` that only works
//     because v1 has a late refcount inserter; shv2 has none, and a lowering-minted `String` would leak.
//   • **The default's EXPRESSION is parsed exactly once, in the file that declares it**, by the ordinary
//     expression parser, against the ordinary scope rules. A call site in ANOTHER FILE could not re-parse
//     it in any case: a shv2 parse is a pure function of one file's `(tokens, filePath, namespace)`, which
//     is precisely what makes the per-file fan-out possible — so the bootstrap's trick of replaying the
//     default's TOKENS at each call site (`CaptureDefaultValueTokens`/`EmitDefaultFromTokens`) is not
//     available here, and would be an architectural regression if it were.
//   • **Dead-function elimination, the linker and the type checker all see it for free**, because they see
//     a `MaxonOp.call`. v1 has to pin its helper in `project.livenessRoots` by hand precisely because its
//     synthesized call is minted at Std level, below the tier DFE walks.
//
// ⚠ **THE CONSEQUENCE, RECORDED SO IT IS NOT LATER READ AS AN OVERSIGHT:** a helper is compiled ONCE, at
// the declaration, so it cannot see anything about the CALL SITE. Caller-location defaults —
// `__line__`/`__file__`, which `specs/source-location-defaults.md` is built on end to end — are therefore
// not expressible under this design and are OUT of scope here. They need the default materialized at the
// caller from caller-side facts, which is a different mechanism, not a missing arm of this one. v1 has the
// identical limitation for the identical reason.
//
// ⚠ There is no constant fast path (v1 splits `intLiteral`/`boolLiteral`/`floatLiteralBits`/`byteLiteral`/
// `enumCase` out of `helper`). One mechanism means one place a default's value is produced: a constant arm
// would have to re-derive literal emission — String interning, array construction, enum tagging, ranged and
// float alias widening — at the CALL SITE, in a second writer, for a value the declaring file's own parser
// already types correctly against the parameter's declared type. The cost is one `call`/`ret` per OMITTED
// argument, which no hot path in a real program pays.
//
// The name is minted by `Project.paramDefaultHelperName` (there, so the `#` join keeps its one speller).
//
// ⭐ **A FUNCTION'S PER-PARAMETER DEFAULTS** — the call-aligned `paramNames` (so a call site can slot a
// LABELLED argument to its position before any `FuncSignature` exists, exactly as `ParamConsumeInfo` does)
// and, per position, the synthesized helper's NAME, or `EmptySourceText` when that parameter has no
// default. The sweep computes it from tokens — the only whole-program input a pure per-file parse may read
// — and records one entry per function that declares AT LEAST ONE default, so a program with none costs a
// single empty map. It travels by NAME with no interner ids, exactly like `ParamConsumeEntry`.
export type ParamDefaultInfo
	export let paramNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let helperNames as ByteArrayArray

	export static function create(paramNames ByteArrayArray, helperNames ByteArrayArray) returns ParamDefaultInfo
		return Self{paramNames: paramNames, helperNames: helperNames}
	end 'create'

	// The helper that produces parameter `index`'s default, or EMPTY when it has none — the door the
	// call-site fill asks per unfilled slot. An index past the recorded list answers EMPTY, the same
	// "absence is the answer" default `ParamConsumeInfo.isConsumed` rests on.
	export function helperFor(index ParamCount) returns ByteArray
		if index >= self.helperNames.count() 'past'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'past'
		return try self.helperNames.get(index) otherwise panic("ParamDefaultInfo.helperFor: helperNames.get OOB at {index} despite index < count")
	end 'helperFor'
end 'ParamDefaultInfo'

export type ParamDefaultEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let info as ParamDefaultInfo

	export static function create(name ByteArray, info ParamDefaultInfo) returns ParamDefaultEntry
		return Self{name: name, info: info}
	end 'create'
end 'ParamDefaultEntry'

export typealias ParamDefaultEntryArray = Array with ParamDefaultEntry
export typealias ParamDefaultMap = Map with (ByteArray, ParamDefaultInfo)

// ⭐ **THE NAMES TWO DIFFERENT DECLARATIONS BOTH CLAIMED, EACH WITH ITS OWN PARAMETER DEFAULTS.** The
// defaults registry is keyed by the name the SOURCE wrote and is last-wins, exactly as `funcParamConsumes`
// is — and a name may be written twice, in two files, which is E3006 and is reported after every file has
// parsed. Until then the loser's own parse reads the WINNER's shape out of this registry, so the position
// its `=` sits at may hold no helper: `Parser.captureParamDefault` asks this set so that collision earns a
// positioned refusal instead of the drain's internal-consistency panic, which is about a different defect
// entirely (the sweep and the real parse disagreeing about ONE declaration) and must stay loud.
typealias CollidingParamDefaultNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The answer to "does this callee declare any parameter default?" — a union so "no recorded entry" is an
// ANSWER (every parameter is mandatory), never confused with an empty-but-present one. `CalleeConsume`'s
// twin, one declaration fact over.
export union CalleeParamDefaults
	defaults(info ParamDefaultInfo)
	allRequired
end 'CalleeParamDefaults'

// One function's `throws E` clause, captured by the declaration sweep (P1.4b) — the argument- and
// call-site dual of its return type, from the SAME token walk. `recordScannedSignature` fills one per
// THROWING function so a call site can decide whether it needs `try` (E3055/E3057) and what type an
// `(e)` binding recovers. Travels by NAME and carries no ids, exactly like `ParamConsumeEntry`.
export type ThrowsClauseEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let clause as ThrowsClause

	export static function create(name ByteArray, clause ThrowsClause) returns ThrowsClauseEntry
		return Self{name: name, clause: clause}
	end 'create'
end 'ThrowsClauseEntry'

export typealias ThrowsClauseEntryArray = Array with ThrowsClauseEntry
export typealias FuncThrowsMap = Map with (ByteArray, ThrowsClause)

// The answer to "does this callee consume any parameter?" — a union so "no recorded entry" is an ANSWER
// (a borrow-only callee), never confused with an empty-but-present one.
export union CalleeConsume
	consumes(info ParamConsumeInfo)
	borrowsAll
end 'CalleeConsume'

// ⭐ **WHICH RUNTIME ENTRY DROPS A MANAGED FIELD OF DECLARED TYPE `fieldType`** — the ONE router shared by
// the parser's value-keyed `decrefCalleeFor` (a top-level owned value's drop) and the struct/union
// destructor cascade (each managed field's drop), so a field moved IN through one classifier is dropped
// through the matching callee and cannot leak (missed drop) or double-free (wrong drop). It generalises the
// union cascade's `StrDecrefName if isString else MmDecrefName` line, which could not reach a nested managed
// struct/union: a `String` drops via `__str_decref`; a struct or boxed union that itself owns managed heap
// drops via ITS `__destruct_<T>` cascade; a scalar-only struct or scalar-only union box takes the trivial
// `__mm_decref` (its box is all it owns).
//
// `typeNames` resolves a `named`/`structRef` field id to a NAME; pass the interner the field type's ids live
// in (the file artifact interner at a construct/extract site, `project.typeNames` at the synthesis site).
// The field is a precondition MANAGED one — every caller asks `structFieldIsManaged`/`payloadClassIsManaged`
// first, so a scalar reaching here is a classification drift and panics rather than mis-routing.
export function managedFieldDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, fieldType MaxonType) returns ByteArray
	let tag = maxonTypeTag(fieldType)
	// ⚠ `tagIsByteRecord`: WHICH DROP a field takes is a question about its record, and a Character's record
	// is a String's. Spelled `tagIsText` a Character field falls through to the named/struct arm and drops
	// through the wrong callee.
	if tagIsByteRecord(tag) 'byteRecord'
		return StrDecrefName
	end 'byteRecord'
	// A nested generic-INSTANCE field (`Box with (Box with String)`, P1.6-B2) is an owned base-struct box:
	// it drops through ITS OWN `__destruct_<instance>` cascade when the instance owns managed heap, else the
	// trivial `__mm_decref` — resolved inside `signatures` over its own registry/interner (a gid is not an
	// interner id), exactly as a struct/union field name is resolved through `managedNameDropCallee`.
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
		return signatures.genericInstanceBoxDropCallee(typeNameIdOf(fieldType))
	end 'instance'
	if not (tagIsStructRef(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.named) 'notManagedRef'
		panic("managedFieldDropCallee: a field of tag '{typeTagName(tag)}' is not a managed heap reference — every caller classifies the field managed first")
	end 'notManagedRef'
	let name = try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) otherwise panic("managedFieldDropCallee: a managed field's type id is not in the interner it was resolved against — pass the interner the field type's ids live in")
	// A GENERIC-INSTANCE-alias field (an `Array`, a `Box`) arrives as a `named` (the sweep recorded the
	// layout before the alias interned) — route it to the instance box drop callee (`__arr_decref` for an
	// `Array`, `__mm_decref`/`__destruct_<T>` for a struct box), exactly as a re-tagged `genericInstance`
	// field would. The gid is `signatures`' own, needing no interner.
	if signatures.isGenericAlias(name) 'genericAlias'
		return signatures.genericInstanceBoxDropCallee(signatures.genericAliasInstance(name))
	end 'genericAlias'
	return managedNameDropCallee(signatures, name: name)
end 'managedFieldDropCallee'

// The drop callee for a managed field NAMED `name` — a declared struct or boxed union. Split from
// `managedFieldDropCallee` so a `structRef` (which is always a struct) and a `named` (struct or union) reach
// the same struct/union lookup once the name is in hand.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES NO INTERNER, AND MUST NOT.** Every question below is keyed by the NAME, and the two that need
// a layout (`structHasManagedField`, `unionHasManagedPayload`) fetch the index's OWN and read it over the
// index's OWN interner. It USED to thread the caller's `typeNames` through to the union arm, which is how a
// `signatures` layout came to be resolved against `project.typeNames` — see `unionHasManagedPayload`.
function managedNameDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures, name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	// ⭐⭐ **A TUPLE TYPEALIAS NAMES THE TUPLE'S OWN STRUCT AND NOTHING ELSE (A3e), so it is resolved
	// ONCE at the head rather than at one of the two lookups below.** A tuple alias mints no identity of its
	// own, so its drop IS the canonical tuple's; and it arrives here under the ALIAS spelling whenever the
	// declaration SWEEP recorded the position before the alias's own file was walked. Every question below
	// is keyed by the name, so resolving here is what keeps the struct arm and the union arm from having to
	// know about aliases at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED, A3e** — `typeIsManaged` must call a tuple-alias field MANAGED (a tuple is a struct box,
	// and without that the field is dropped by nobody and the program exits 101 on the leak gate). Widening it
	// and leaving this alone turned that leak into the `undeclared` PANIC at the bottom of this function, on a
	// program the other walk order compiled and ran — a strictly worse answer than either.
	let declaredName = signatures.denotedAggregateName(name)
	// ⭐ **A `CharacterSet` IS A COMPILER-OWNED AGGREGATE AND TAKES A COMPILER-OWNED DROP** (P1.8 Slice D),
	// exactly as an `Array` takes `__arr_decref` and a `Set` takes `__set_decref`. Asked BEFORE the
	// declared-struct lookup: its layout IS registered (it needs a nominal identity) and it DOES own a
	// managed field, so the generic route below would answer `__destruct___CharacterSet` — a symbol
	// `installStructDestructors` never builds, because it walks `project.structTypes` and a synthesized type
	// is in no file's declarations. A link failure rather than a wrong answer, but the honest fix is to say
	// which drop this type takes.
	if declaredName.equals(CharacterSetTypeName) 'characterSet'
		return CharSetDecrefName
	end 'characterSet'
	// ⭐⭐ **A `__ManagedFile` OWNS AN OS HANDLE, WHICH IS NOT MEMORY — SO ITS DROP MUST CLOSE, NOT JUST
	// FREE (R4.2, and this line IS the RAII destructor).** Asked here, beside `CharacterSet`, because the
	// shape is identical: a compiler-owned aggregate whose drop is a compiler-owned entry point rather
	// than a synthesized `__destruct_<T>` cascade.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT COULD NOT BE THE CASCADE, and that was measured rather than assumed.** `_handle` is a scalar
	// `int` field, so `structFieldIsManaged` is false and `installStructDestructors` skips the type
	// outright — nothing is synthesized. Widening "managed field" to cover it would be a lie every other
	// reader of that predicate would then act on, and `emitNullGuardedDrop`'s shape is wrong for it anyway:
	// it loads the FIELD and calls `callee(field)`, while a close needs the BOX (it zeroes `_handle` to stay
	// idempotent). `__mf_destruct(box)` is the refcount-checked teardown that does both.
	//
	// ⇒ Answering `MmDecrefName` here — which is what R4.1 did — frees the eight bytes and leaks the OS
	// handle for the process lifetime, invisibly to the exit-101 gate, because a handle is not memory.
	// ONE line covers both doors: a `var f as __ManagedFile` FIELD through the struct cascade, and a
	// top-level `let f = __ManagedFile.openRead(…)` through `Parser.decrefCalleeFor`'s `structRef` arm.
	if declaredName.equals(ManagedFileTypeName) 'managedFile'
		return ManagedFileDestructName
	end 'managedFile'
	// ⭐ **A `__ManagedDirectory` OWNS A SEARCH HANDLE *AND* A SECOND ALLOCATION (R4.3)** — so its drop must
	// close the search and free the find block, neither of which the generic `__mm_decref` of an 8-byte box
	// would do. Every argument the file line above makes applies unchanged: `_block` is a scalar `int` field,
	// so `structFieldIsManaged` is false and `installStructDestructors` synthesizes nothing; and
	// `emitNullGuardedDrop`'s "load the FIELD, call `callee(field)`" shape is wrong for a close that must
	// zero the handle to stay idempotent. `__md_destruct(box)` is the refcount-checked teardown that does
	// all three. Answering `MmDecrefName` here would free eight bytes and leak BOTH the 336-byte block and
	// the OS search handle — the second of which the exit-101 gate cannot see at all.
	if declaredName.equals(ManagedDirectoryTypeName) 'managedDirectory'
		return ManagedDirectoryDestructName
	end 'managedDirectory'
	if signatures.containsStruct(declaredName) 'struct'
		if signatures.structHasManagedField(declaredName) 'managedStruct'
			return destructorSymbol(declaredName)
		end 'managedStruct'
		return MmDecrefName
	end 'struct'
	// ⭐ **BY NAME, exactly as the struct arm above is** — and through the SAME `unionBoxDropCallee` a boxed
	// union's scope-exit drop takes, so a union FIELD and a union BINDING cannot be dropped through
	// different callees. See `unionHasManagedPayload` for the reachable compiler panic the layout-and-
	// foreign-interner spelling this replaces produced.
	match signatures.enumOf(declaredName) 'union'
		declared then return signatures.unionBoxDropCallee(declaredName)
		// A managed field that is neither struct nor enum cannot occur: `structFieldIsManaged` classified it
		// managed, and its only managed non-String forms are a declared struct and a boxed union.
		undeclared then panic("managedNameDropCallee: managed field '{String.from(declaredName)}' is neither a declared struct nor a declared enum/union — the caller classified it managed")
	end 'union'
end 'managedNameDropCallee'

// ⭐ **HOW A MANAGED FIELD OF DECLARED TYPE `fieldType` IS DEEP-CLONED (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b).** The exact
// STRUCTURAL DUAL of `managedFieldDropCallee`: a value cloned through the strategy this returns is dropped
// through the callee `managedFieldDropCallee` returns for the SAME type, so the cloned set and the dropped set
// agree by construction (a clone leaks if it is not dropped, double-frees if it is dropped twice — one
// classifier decides both). shv2 is move-only, so an independent copy is a per-element DEEP CLONE, never an
// incref.
export union ManagedCloneStrategy
	// A `(box) -> newBox` clone callee invocable by a plain 1-arg call OR through `callIndirect(fn, box)` (a
	// `funcAddr`): a String → `__str_clone`; a struct / boxed union → its `__clone_<T>` (managed OR scalar-only
	// — a fresh box is needed either way, where the drop side takes `__mm_decref` for a scalar-only box); a
	// TRIVIAL-element Array → `__arr_clone` (its COW clone is an independently-droppable copy). This is the ONLY
	// strategy an ARRAY ELEMENT may carry, because an element cloner is invoked through `callIndirect(fn, elem)`.
	direct(callee ByteArray)
	// A MANAGED-element Array FIELD: `__arr_clone_managed(field, funcAddr(elementClone))`, where `elementClone`
	// is the element's own `direct` clone callee. A 2-arg call, so it is reachable only as a struct/union FIELD
	// (the synthesizer emits the funcAddr), never as a top-level array element (which must be `direct`).
	managedArray(elementClone ByteArray)
	// Not deep-cloneable by this rung's mechanism — a non-Array generic instance (`Box with String`, whose
	// per-instance cloner is a later slice) or a managed-element Array whose element is not itself `direct`
	// (an array-of-managed-arrays). The front end rejects such an element with a positioned E2015.
	unsupported
end 'ManagedCloneStrategy'

// The clone strategy for a managed field of declared type `fieldType`. Mirrors `managedFieldDropCallee`'s
// dispatch exactly: `typeNames` resolves a `named`/`structRef` id to a NAME; pass the interner the field
// type's ids live in (the file artifact interner at a call site, `project.typeNames` at the synthesis site).
// The field is a precondition MANAGED one, so a scalar reaching here is a classification drift and panics.
export function managedFieldCloneStrategy(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, fieldType MaxonType) returns ManagedCloneStrategy
	let tag = maxonTypeTag(fieldType)
	// `tagIsByteRecord`, mirroring `managedFieldDropCallee`: a Character clones by the same fused-record
	// deep copy a String does, because it IS one.
	if tagIsByteRecord(tag) 'byteRecord'
		return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(StrCloneName)
	end 'byteRecord'
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
		return instanceCloneStrategy(signatures, id: typeNameIdOf(fieldType))
	end 'instance'
	if not (tagIsStructRef(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.named) 'notManagedRef'
		panic("managedFieldCloneStrategy: a field of tag '{typeTagName(tag)}' is not a managed heap reference — every caller classifies the field managed first")
	end 'notManagedRef'
	// Resolved through `denotedAggregateName` for `managedFieldDropCallee`'s reason, and it must be the SAME
	// resolution: a value cloned by this strategy is dropped by the callee that one returns, so a name the two
	// resolved differently would clone one type and drop another.
	let name = signatures.denotedAggregateName(try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) otherwise panic("managedFieldCloneStrategy: a managed field's type id is not in the interner it was resolved against — pass the interner the field type's ids live in"))
	if signatures.isGenericAlias(name) 'genericAlias'
		return instanceCloneStrategy(signatures, id: signatures.genericAliasInstance(name))
	end 'genericAlias'
	return managedNameCloneStrategy(signatures, name: name)
end 'managedFieldCloneStrategy'

// The clone strategy for a managed field NAMED `name` — a declared struct or boxed union clones through its
// per-type `__clone_<T>`. A scalar-only box still takes `__clone_<T>` (not the drop side's `__mm_decref`): the
// clone MUST allocate a fresh independent box, which only a synthesized cloner does.
function managedNameCloneStrategy(signatures ProgramSignatures, name ByteArray) returns ManagedCloneStrategy
	if signatures.containsStruct(name) 'struct'
		return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(clonerSymbol(name))
	end 'struct'
	match signatures.enumOf(name) 'union'
		declared then return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(clonerSymbol(name))
		undeclared then panic("managedNameCloneStrategy: managed field '{String.from(name)}' is neither a declared struct nor a declared enum/union — the caller classified it managed")
	end 'union'
end 'managedNameCloneStrategy'

// The clone strategy for a generic-INSTANCE field — an `Array` (`__arr_clone` for a trivial element,
// `__arr_clone_managed` for a managed one) or, for any other instance (`Box with String`), `unsupported` (its
// per-instance cloner is a later slice). The managed element's own strategy comes back through the METHOD
// `arrayElementCloneStrategy` (which resolves the element in the index's own interner), so this free function
// never has to reach the index's private `typeNames`.
function instanceCloneStrategy(signatures ProgramSignatures, id GenericInstanceId) returns ManagedCloneStrategy
	if not signatures.isArrayInstance(id) 'notArray'
		return ManagedCloneStrategy.unsupported
	end 'notArray'
	if not signatures.arrayElementIsManaged(id) 'trivialElement'
		return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(ArrCloneName)
	end 'trivialElement'
	// A managed-element array field clones each element through the element's OWN `(box) -> newBox` callee; if
	// that element is not itself `direct` (an array-of-managed-arrays), the whole field is unsupported here.
	match signatures.arrayElementCloneStrategy(id) 'element'
		direct(elementClone) then return ManagedCloneStrategy.managedArray(elementClone)
		managedArray then return ManagedCloneStrategy.unsupported
		unsupported then return ManagedCloneStrategy.unsupported
	end 'element'
end 'instanceCloneStrategy'

// The single `(box) -> newBox` clone callee of a `direct` strategy. Precondition: the caller has classified the
// element deep-cloneable through the FULL-GRAPH `typeSupportsDeepClone(asElement: true)` (via
// `genericInstanceOpaqueArrayElementIsCopyable` or the concrete copy gate `arrayElementSupportsDeepClone`), which
// is `direct` at the TOP LEVEL for every deep-cloneable element — so a `managedArray`/`unsupported` strategy
// reaching here is an invariant break and panics. (The full-graph gate is the RIGHT precondition, not a mere
// top-level `direct` check: a struct/union that is `direct` at the top but owns a non-clonable field must NOT
// reach a stamp — see `genericInstanceOpaqueArrayElementIsCopyable`.)
export function directCloneCalleeOf(strategy ManagedCloneStrategy) returns ByteArray
	match strategy 'strategy'
		direct(callee) then return callee
		managedArray then panic("directCloneCalleeOf: a `managedArray` strategy has no single-function clone callee — the caller must gate on typeSupportsDeepClone(asElement: true)")
		unsupported then panic("directCloneCalleeOf: an `unsupported` strategy has no clone callee — the caller must gate on typeSupportsDeepClone(asElement: true)")
	end 'strategy'
end 'directCloneCalleeOf'

// --- Generic instances (P1.6-A) ---

// The prefix a compiled instance name is minted behind when a declaration already claims the bare one
// (`reservedIfDeclared`). It is the SAME `__` the language reserves — `E2051` refuses to declare a name
// starting with it.
//
// ⚠ **BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT THAT RESERVATION BUYS, BECAUSE IT IS NOT THE OBVIOUS HALF.** Disjointness
// from the DECLARED names is bought by `reservedIfDeclared`'s loop, which re-probes until the candidate
// is unclaimed and would hold for any prefix at all. What only `__` buys is disjointness from every
// OTHER INSTANCE's compiled name — the half nothing probes — and it buys it because a compiled name
// always BEGINS with its base name and E2051 bars a `__` base: no bare compiled name can start with the
// prefix, so a minted one is never some other instantiation's bare one. Pick a prefix the language does
// NOT reserve and that fails silently: `XBox_String` is a name `XBox with String` compiles to on its
// own, and the pair check would report E3006 against a legal program. (E2051 also bounds the loop at one
// re-mint on anything that compiles, which is a cost property, not a safety one.)
//
// It is a third module-local copy of that one language rule, beside `Parser.ReservedNamePrefix` (which
// FORBIDS a user declaration from taking it) and `MmRuntime.CompilerInternalPrefix` (which RECOGNIZES an
// emitted callee by it), and for the reason Parser's own comment states: the three answer different
// questions at different tiers — forbidding, recognizing, and here MINTING — and a signature index owning
// the parser's reservation, or the reverse, would be the worse coupling. The language rule binds them,
// not a shared symbol. A drift between the three is LOUD rather than silent: this one's value reaches
// every emitted per-type symbol of a contested instance, so changing it alone moves goldens, and
// emptying it hangs the loop.
let ReservedTypeNamePrefix = b"__"

// The name of the builtin `Array` generic type (`Array with Integer`). Shared by the parser's
// `Base with Args` check (which treats `Array` as a one-parameter generic without a `type` declaration)
// and every `Array`-instance query — one speller, so the two cannot drift. See `isArrayBaseName`.
export let ArrayBuiltinBaseName = b"Array"

// The element type name of a `b"…"` byte-string literal's `Array with Byte` instance — the same `Byte`
// spelling a user's `typealias Byte = int(0 to u8.max)` uses, so a byte-string's gid matches a
// `ByteArray`-typed value's gid (type identity). A bare `named` reference: the element's own resolution
// is never needed (a byte-string stamps `element_size = 1` directly and never reads `arrayElementSize`).
let ByteElementTypeName = b"Byte"

// The single type parameter `Array` declares (`Array with Element`) — the arity `checkGenericInstance`
// validates a `Array with …` against.
export let ArrayTypeParamCount = 1 as FieldIndex

// The name of the builtin `Set` generic type (`Set with Element`, P1.7b slice A) — the hash-table twin of
// `ArrayBuiltinBaseName`. `Set` has no `type Set uses Element` declaration (shv2 reads no stdlib; its
// runtime is synthesized like `Array`'s), so every `genericInstance*` query that would read a base
// `StructLayout` (`baseLayoutOf`, box size, per-instance drop) special-cases a `Set` instance exactly as it
// does an `Array` one — its box is the fixed `SetRecordBytes` fused record and it drops through
// `__set_decref`. One speller, shared by the parser's `Base with Args` check and every `Set`-instance query.
export let SetBuiltinBaseName = b"Set"

// The single type parameter `Set` declares (`Set with Element`) — the arity `checkGenericInstance`
// validates a `Set with …` against, the `Array` twin.
export let SetTypeParamCount = 1 as FieldIndex

// The `element_size@24` of a MANAGED-element array (P1.7 slice 3) — a managed element (String / struct /
// union / instance / nested Array) is a HEAP BOX addressed by a POINTER, so a slot holds the 8-byte pointer,
// NOT the element's full logical size. A trivial element is stored inline and strides by its own size (an
// `int` 8, a `Byte = int(0 to u8.max)` 1). This is why `arrayElementSize` cannot simply be the source
// `sizeof`: the STORAGE width of a managed element is the pointer — a `String` element's slot is 8, where
// its `sizeof` is the 48-byte record — and the drop walk / accessors move exactly that pointer.
let ManagedArrayElementBytes = 8 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THE STORAGE WIDTH OF A `named` ARRAY ELEMENT NO FILE DECLARES AS A RANGED ALIAS** — the MISS arm of
// `trivialElementStorageBytes`'s registry probe. It is a function and not a bare fallback because a miss
// means TWO different things, and only one of them is the machine word:
//
//   • **`Byte`** — the `Array with Byte` instance a `b"…"` literal MINTS FOR ITSELF
//     (`internArrayByteInstance`) in a program that declares no such alias. No user type can name that
//     instance (`Array with Byte` over an undeclared `Byte` does not resolve), so the literal's own record
//     is the only one there is — and it is stamped byte-PACKED by construction (`ByteStringElementSize`).
//     Answering the machine word here would leave this door and that record disagreeing about ONE
//     instance's stride, which is the two-producer shape `arrayElementSize` exists to end.
//   • **anything else** — an enum or a boxed union's ordinal. Its range is not an `int(lo to hi)`
//     declaration, so it misses the same probe, and it deliberately keeps the machine word
//     (`specs-shv2/array-enum-element-size.md` pins that stride; narrowing it is its own rung).
function undeclaredNamedElementStorageBytes(name ByteArray) returns ByteOffset
	if name.equals(ByteElementTypeName) or name.equals(StringByteViewElementName) 'bytePacked'
		return ByteStringElementSize as ByteOffset
	end 'bytePacked'

	return primitiveTypeByteSize(ValueTypeTag.named)
end 'undeclaredNamedElementStorageBytes'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ELEMENT OF A STRING'S BYTE VIEW (`String.addressableBytes()`, BATCH2 slice 1) — AND WHY IT IS
// NOT `Byte`.** `__str_bytes_view` publishes a record over a String's OWN buffer, and a String's bytes are
// bytes: its `element_size@24` is `StringElementSize`, 1, always. `Array with Byte` cannot carry that,
// because `Byte` is a name a USER PROGRAM may declare — `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` makes
// `internArrayByteInstance()` stride TWO — and the view would then be a static type and a runtime record
// disagreeing about one instance's stride, which is the two-producer shape `arrayElementSize` exists to end.
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED, and it is why this name exists at all**: with the view typed `Array with Byte`,
// `specs-shv2/bytearray-element-size.md`'s two wide-`Byte` cases went red with
// `E3005 argument type mismatch for 'managed': expected '__ManagedMemory', got 'Array_Byte'` raised INSIDE
// `stdlib/URL.maxon` — a whitelisted module is parsed by every program, so a user's `Byte` alias broke the
// stdlib. A `b"…"` literal meets the identical wall and is REFUSED for it (E2015, same spec); the stdlib's
// own byte reader cannot be refused, so it takes an element the user cannot reach instead.
//
// It is `__`-RESERVED, so no program can declare it and `undeclaredNamedElementStorageBytes` is the only
// answer for its width — the same reason `StringIndexTypeName` wears one. This is `parseDecodedView`'s rule
// applied to bytes: a compiler-synthesized view's stride is the compiler's to state, never a function of
// which aliases the program happens to declare.
let StringByteViewElementName = b"__StringByte"

// ⭐⭐ **THE COMPILER-OWNED `StringIndex` TYPE (P1.8 slice B2), AND WHY IT WEARS A RESERVED NAME.**
//
// The reference declares it as an ordinary two-field `type` (`stdlib/String.maxon:917-946`: `var charIdx as
// GraphemeIndex`, `var bytePosition as BytePos`), so shv2 registers an ordinary two-field `StructLayout`
// and gets the whole struct-value machinery for it — a binding's type, aggregate identity on a reassign,
// and a scope-exit drop through the trivial-struct `__mm_decref` — with no new `ValueTypeTag` and no new
// ownership rule. shv2 has struct VALUES; what it does not have is a `type` declaration for this one, and
// this is that declaration, made by the compiler.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NAMED `__StringIndex`, NOT `StringIndex`, AND THAT IS A SAFETY PROPERTY RATHER THAN A STYLE.**
// The registry is a MAP keyed by name: a user program declaring its own `type StringIndex` would land in
// the same bucket, and whichever write ran last would win — after which `s.startIndex()` mints a 16-byte
// box the parser then reads through the USER's field table, at the user's offsets and widths. Silent
// memory corruption, decided by file order. The `__` prefix makes the contest unrepresentable instead of
// merely unlikely: `Parser.requireUnreservedName` refuses (E2051) every declaration of a `__`-prefixed
// name, so no source can spell this one. It is the SAME reserved space `mangleGenericInstance` mints a
// contested instance name into, applied one level up — and the cost is nil, because the corpus never
// writes `StringIndex` in a type position at all (measured: zero occurrences across `specs/`). A
// `StringIndex` is only ever a value flowing from `startIndex`/`endIndex`/`findFirst`/`findLast` into
// `slice`/`charIndex()`/`bytePos()`/`==`, none of which name the type.
// ⇒ The consequence to know: a `StringIndex` cannot cross a function boundary, because a parameter or
// return type would have to name it. The reference allows that and shv2 does not, until the type gains a
// user-visible spelling and, with it, a builtin-redeclaration diagnostic to defend it.
export let StringIndexTypeName = b"__StringIndex"

// Its two fields, in the reference's own declaration ORDER — which is the layout, since
// `StructLayout.offsetOfField` derives an offset from the index. Both are `var` in the reference and
// NEITHER is exported: the reference reaches them through the `charIndex()`/`bytePos()` accessors, and a
// non-exported field is E3014 from anywhere but the declaring type, which here is nowhere. Immutable
// because nothing writes a `StringIndex` after it is minted.
let StringIndexCharIdxFieldName = b"charIdx"
let StringIndexBytePosFieldName = b"bytePosition"

// ⭐ **THE LAYOUT, AND THE CHECK THAT IT MATCHES THE BOX THE RUNTIME BUILDS.** Two readers describe this
// one shape and they must not drift: the PARSER reads the fields through `emitFieldLoad`, which derives
// offsets from this layout, while `__strix_make` WRITES them through `StringIndexRuntime`'s own
// constants. The assertions below are what make that pair a checked invariant rather than a promise —
// `buildStringRecordBytes`'s `appendRecordField` discipline, applied to a struct box.
function stringIndexLayout() returns StructLayout
	var layout = StructLayout.create(StringIndexTypeName)
	layout.addField(StringIndexCharIdxFieldName, declaredType: MaxonType.integer, mutable: false, exported: false, defaultValue: FieldDefault.none, declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface)
	layout.addField(StringIndexBytePosFieldName, declaredType: MaxonType.integer, mutable: false, exported: false, defaultValue: FieldDefault.none, declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface)

	if layout.offsetOfField(StringIndexCharIdxFieldIndex) != StringIndexCharIdxOffset or layout.offsetOfField(StringIndexBytePosFieldIndex) != StringIndexBytePosOffset 'offsetDrift'
		panic("stringIndexLayout: the struct layout puts `charIdx`/`bytePosition` at {layout.offsetOfField(StringIndexCharIdxFieldIndex)}/{layout.offsetOfField(StringIndexBytePosFieldIndex)} but `__strix_make` writes them at {StringIndexCharIdxOffset}/{StringIndexBytePosOffset} — the parser's field reads and the runtime's field writes have drifted")
	end 'offsetDrift'
	if layout.sizeBytes() as ParsedInt != StringIndexBoxBytes 'sizeDrift'
		panic("stringIndexLayout: the struct layout is {layout.sizeBytes()} bytes but `__strix_make` allocates {StringIndexBoxBytes} — the box the runtime builds and the box the parser reads have drifted")
	end 'sizeDrift'
	return layout
end 'stringIndexLayout'

// The two fields' declaration indices — what `Parser.dispatchStringIndexMethod` hands `emitFieldLoad`,
// and what the drift check above reads the offsets at. Named because an index IS the identity here (see
// `StructLayout.offsetOfField`), so a bare `0`/`1` at a read site is exactly the magic value the rule
// against them is for.
export let StringIndexCharIdxFieldIndex = 0 as FieldIndex
export let StringIndexBytePosFieldIndex = 1 as FieldIndex

// ⭐ **THE COMPILER-OWNED `CharacterSet` TYPE (P1.8 Slice D).** Registered under the RESERVED name
// `__CharacterSet` for the reason `__StringIndex` is (see that constant): a user `type CharacterSet` and
// this synthesized layout would otherwise contest one bucket, and the loser's box would be read through
// the winner's field table. `Parser.requireUnreservedName` refuses (E2051) every `__`-prefixed
// declaration, so no source can spell this one.
// ⇒ The consequence to know, and it is `StringIndex`'s: a `CharacterSet` cannot cross a function
// boundary, because a parameter or return type would have to NAME it. Every corpus use is a value flowing
// from a `CharacterSet.<accessor>()` straight into `contains`/`trim` or a local `let`, which names nothing.
export let CharacterSetTypeName = b"__CharacterSet"

// The user-visible spelling -- what a source `CharacterSet.letters()` writes, and the ONLY door the type
// is reachable through. Distinct from the reserved registry name above, and deliberately so.
export let CharacterSetBuiltinName = b"CharacterSet"

// ⭐ **THE COMPILER-OWNED `__ManagedFile` TYPE (R4.1).** An 8-byte box holding one raw OS file HANDLE,
// with a runtime-owned destructor that closes it on drop — `specs/managed-file.md`'s "Type Structure"
// states the single `_handle` field and the RAII contract.
//
// ⚠ **UNLIKE `__StringIndex` AND `__CharacterSet`, THE REGISTRY NAME AND THE USER-FACING NAME ARE THE
// SAME BYTES, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS TYPE CAN DO WHAT THEY CANNOT.** Those two are registered
// under a reserved `__`-prefixed name while the source spells a bare one, and `CharacterSetTypeName`'s
// comment draws the consequence: such a type "cannot cross a function boundary, because a parameter or
// return type would have to NAME it". The corpus REQUIRES this one to cross — `specs/managed-file.md`
// writes `path __ManagedMemory` as a declared parameter and `export var file as __ManagedFile` as a
// field — and it can, because the name a user writes IS the name the layout is registered under, so
// there is no second spelling to fail to resolve.
// ⇒ **The `__` prefix is therefore NOT a barrier here, and that was MEASURED rather than assumed:**
// `requireUnreservedName`'s E2051 refuses a `__`-prefixed DECLARATION (`type __Foo`), not a `__`-prefixed
// name in a TYPE POSITION. A field declared `as __StringIndex` today reports `E3011 Unknown type`, not
// E2051 — it fails to RESOLVE, and resolving is exactly what registering the layout fixes.
export let ManagedFileTypeName = b"__ManagedFile"

// ⭐ **`__ManagedMemory` IS AN `Array`, NOT A TYPE OF ITS OWN (R4.1, generalized by R4.4).** Registered as
// a builtin GENERIC ALIAS — the same door `CharSet` goes through (`registerCharacterSetType`) — bound to
// the exact instance `internArrayByteInstance()` mints, which is the instance `"…".toByteArray()` already
// yields and the one a `b"…"` literal already has. So a `path __ManagedMemory` parameter and the argument
// handed to it are ONE type by construction, and `.managed` is an identity rather than a conversion.
//
// ⭐⭐ **R4.4 KEPT THE BINDING AND MADE IT ELEMENT-AGNOSTIC, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF THAT RUNG.** The
// reference declares `__ManagedMemory` generic over ONE parameter named `Element` (v1
// `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1608-1621`; bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:1254` + `:1391`) and implements `Array` ON it
// (`stdlib/Array.maxon:104-106`: `typealias ElementMemory = __ManagedMemory with Element`, `export var
// managed as ElementMemory`), so `__ManagedMemory with T` and `Array with T` describe ONE record. shv2
// SYNTHESIZES that record instead of compiling `stdlib/Array.maxon`, so for shv2 they ARE one
// `GenericInstanceId` — which is what makes `.managed` an identity read at EVERY element type and leaves
// nothing to reconcile at `String.init`, `.toByteArray()`, `valueIsByteArray` or a `__ManagedFile`
// parameter. A distinct generic base would mint a second id at each of those.
// ⇒ The bare spelling (no `with`) keeps meaning the BYTE instance, because that is what every
// `__ManagedFile` signature declares and what a path buffer is.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO SURFACES OF ONE RECORD ARE THEREFORE TOLD APART BY PROVENANCE, NOT BY TYPE** — see
// `Parser.valueIsBufferSurface`. The reference separates `Array.count()` from `ByteMemory.length()` with
// two TYPES; shv2 has one, so it carries "this value denotes the BUFFER" on the value instead. That is the
// price of the binding, and it is the smaller one: the alternative is two ids for one record.
export let ManagedMemoryTypeName = b"__ManagedMemory"

// ⭐⭐ **ONE DECLARED TYPE POSITION'S PRE-ERASURE `__ManagedMemory` SPELLING (A2j / A2m).** A parameter's
// annotation, a struct field's, a return clause's: each is a place where the SOURCE said something no
// `MaxonType` can still say, because `parseTypeReference` folds `__ManagedMemory` into the very
// `genericInstance(Array with Byte)` a `ByteArray` resolves to (`registerManagedFileType`).
//
// TWO facts per position, because a declaration can name the buffer as the position itself or one container
// in, and each decides a different value's member roster:
//   • the position IS the buffer                  — `m __ManagedMemory`                            (A2j)
//   • the position's ARRAY ELEMENT is the buffer  — `a BufArray`, `BufArray = Array with __ManagedMemory`
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND A TUPLE POSITION CARRIES ONE WHOLE SURFACE PER SLOT, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A TREE AND NOT A
// MASK.** A2m first shipped it as a flat 62-bit mask, one BIT per slot — which meant a slot could say "I am a
// buffer" and nothing else, so `(__ManagedMemory, Int)` worked while `(BufArray, Int)` and
// `((__ManagedMemory, Int), Int)` did not, for no reason a reader of the language could state. A slot is a
// declared position like any other; it gets the same payload as any other, recursively.
//
// ⚠ **AND THAT REMOVES THE WIDTH QUESTION RATHER THAN ANSWERING IT AGAIN.** The mask had an arity ceiling
// (62) and needed a REFUSAL to keep a 63rd slot from silently losing its bit — a lost bit being the wrong
// member roster with no diagnostic either way. A tree has one node per named position and no ceiling in
// either direction: no arity limit, no depth limit, nothing to truncate, and so nothing to refuse. The
// ceiling and its refusal are gone.
//
// ⚠ **THE TREE MAY NOT RIDE THE TUPLE'S `StructLayout` INSTEAD.** `(__ManagedMemory, Int)` and
// `(ByteArray, Int)` canonicalize to ONE interned tuple sharing ONE layout, so a surface written onto that
// layout would hand the buffer's roster to BOTH spellings — a wrong ACCEPTANCE, which no diagnostic reports
// and which `error.a-tuple-of-byte-arrays-keeps-the-array-surface` exists to hold shut. The declaration
// carries the fact and the VALUE carries it onward (`Parser.bufferSurfaceSlots`).

// ⭐ **ONE NODE OF THAT TREE, PACKED INTO A WORD.** Bit 0 is the position itself, bit 1 its array element,
// and everything above is the number of TUPLE SLOTS the node has — zero for a position that is not a tuple.
export typealias DeclaredSurfaceNode = int(0 to u64.max)

// ⭐⭐ **THE TREE, FLATTENED PRE-ORDER: node 0 is the position, and each node's slots follow it in order,
// each with its own subtree.** `(A, (B, C))` is `[tuple(2), A, tuple(2), B, C]`.
//
// ⚠ **FLAT AND PRE-ORDER RATHER THAN A RECORD THAT POINTS AT ITS CHILDREN, WHICH IS THIS COMPILER'S OWN
// ESTABLISHED SHAPE FOR EXACTLY THIS** — see `GenericArgNodeArray`, which flattens a nested `Base with (…)`
// the same way. It buys two things a record cannot: the element is an inline WORD, so a tree costs one
// allocation however deep it is; and a PLAIN position is the shared EMPTY tree, so it costs none at all.
// That matters because every door carries this fact as a column — one per parameter of every function in
// the program, one per field of every type — and all but a handful of them are plain.
export typealias DeclaredSurface = Array with DeclaredSurfaceNode

// One declared surface per declared POSITION, in declaration order — a struct's fields
// (`StructLayout.fieldDeclaredSurfaces`) and a function's parameters (`Parser.parseFunctionParameters`).
export typealias DeclaredSurfaceArray = Array with DeclaredSurface

// ⭐ **A POSITION THAT NAMED THE BUFFER NOWHERE — AN EMPTY TREE, AND THE ONE SHARED INSTANCE OF IT.** It is
// the answer for every type in a program that never writes the alias, so it is handed out rather than built:
// `sharedEmptyWhereConstraints`' pattern, stored in record fields and pushed into columns exactly as that one
// is. Nothing ever writes it — every builder that finds something allocates its own tree first.
export let sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface = DeclaredSurface.create()

// Bit 0: the position itself was spelled `__ManagedMemory`.
export let SurfaceBitIsBuffer = 1 as DeclaredSurfaceNode

// Bit 1: the position named a generic alias whose ELEMENT was spelled `__ManagedMemory`.
export let SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer = 2 as DeclaredSurfaceNode

// Everything above the two flag bits is the node's SLOT COUNT. THE one speller of the shift, shared by the
// builder (`Parser.declaredSurfaceAt`) and the readers below, so the two cannot come to disagree about where
// a count ends and a flag begins.
let SurfaceSlotCountShift = 2 as DeclaredSurfaceNode

// The node a TUPLE position contributes: no flags of its own, and `slotCount` children behind it. A tuple
// value is an ordinary record whatever its slots denote, which is why its own two flag bits stay clear.
export function tupleSurfaceNode(slotCount FieldIndex) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
	// The count is bound rather than cast inline: the bootstrap reads a cast nested inside a shift as already
	// hashed and rejects it as unneeded (E3010), the same false positive `mixDefaultLiteral` binds a local for.
	let widened = slotCount as DeclaredSurfaceNode
	return widened shl SurfaceSlotCountShift
end 'tupleSurfaceNode'

// Does this position name the buffer nowhere at all? The empty tree, which is what every plain declaration
// gets — asked wherever a caller wants to skip the whole mechanism for the overwhelmingly common case.
export function surfaceIsPlain(surface DeclaredSurface) returns bool
	return surface.count() == 0
end 'surfaceIsPlain'

// Was the position ITSELF spelled `__ManagedMemory`?
export function surfaceIsBuffer(surface DeclaredSurface) returns bool
	return (surfaceRootNode(surface) and SurfaceBitIsBuffer) != 0
end 'surfaceIsBuffer'

// Did the position name a generic alias whose ELEMENT was spelled `__ManagedMemory`?
export function surfaceElementIsBuffer(surface DeclaredSurface) returns bool
	return (surfaceRootNode(surface) and SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer) != 0
end 'surfaceElementIsBuffer'

// How many TUPLE SLOTS this position has — zero for a position that is not a tuple, and zero for a plain
// one. It is what a reader asks before `surfaceSlot`, so a non-tuple never reaches the walk.
export function surfaceSlotCount(surface DeclaredSurface) returns FieldIndex
	return surfaceNodeSlotCount(surfaceRootNode(surface))
end 'surfaceSlotCount'

// How many slots ONE node has. THE one decoder of the count field, so the root reader above and the
// pre-order subtree walk below cannot come to disagree about where a count ends and a flag begins.
function surfaceNodeSlotCount(node DeclaredSurfaceNode) returns FieldIndex
	return node shr SurfaceSlotCountShift
end 'surfaceNodeSlotCount'

// ⭐ **THE SURFACE OF TUPLE SLOT `slot` — A WHOLE SURFACE, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE TREE.** The slot's own
// subtree, copied out so the result is rooted at node 0 and every reader can be handed a `DeclaredSurface`
// with no index alongside it. It allocates, and only here: a caller reaches this line only for a slot of a
// tuple that actually named the buffer somewhere.
//
// `slot` is a precondition, not a case — every caller asked `surfaceSlotCount` first.
export function surfaceSlot(surface DeclaredSurface, slot FieldIndex) returns DeclaredSurface
	let start = surfaceSlotStart(surface, slot: slot)
	let stop = start + surfaceSubtreeSize(surface, at: start)
	var out = DeclaredSurface.create()
	for i in start upto stop 'eachNode'
		out.push(surfaceNodeAt(surface, at: i))
	end 'eachNode'
	return out
end 'surfaceSlot'

// ⭐ **THE SAME SURFACE WITH ITS ELEMENT BIT SET.** The one WRITER outside the parser's builder, and it
// COPIES rather than mutating: the tree it is handed is the one stored on a `StructLayout` or in the
// whole-program return table, shared by every reader of that declaration, and setting a bit in place would
// teach one file's read to every other file's. Its callers are the two doors that must DERIVE the element
// bit rather than store it — see `ProgramSignatures.declaredElementIsBufferSurface`.
export function surfaceWithElementBuffer(surface DeclaredSurface) returns DeclaredSurface
	var out = DeclaredSurface.create()
	out.push(surfaceRootNode(surface) or SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer)
	for i in 1 upto surface.count() 'eachRemainingNode'
		out.push(surfaceNodeAt(surface, at: i))
	end 'eachRemainingNode'
	return out
end 'surfaceWithElementBuffer'

// Node `at` of the tree. A precondition, not a case: every index handed here came from a walk that started
// at node 0 and advanced by whole subtrees.
function surfaceNodeAt(surface DeclaredSurface, at FieldIndex) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
	return try surface.get(at) otherwise panic("surfaceNodeAt: node {at} is out of range for a {surface.count()}-node declared surface — every index came from a pre-order walk of this same tree")
end 'surfaceNodeAt'

// Node 0, or an all-clear node for the shared EMPTY tree. The empty tree is the plain answer, so reading it
// as a node with no flags and no slots is what makes every accessor above answer plainly without its own
// emptiness test.
function surfaceRootNode(surface DeclaredSurface) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
	if surfaceIsPlain(surface) 'noNodes'
		return 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
	end 'noNodes'

	return surfaceNodeAt(surface, at: 0)
end 'surfaceRootNode'

// How many nodes the subtree rooted at `at` occupies — itself plus every descendant. THE one walk that knows
// pre-order layout, so `surfaceSlotStart` and `surfaceSlot` cannot come to disagree about where a subtree
// ends.
function surfaceSubtreeSize(surface DeclaredSurface, at FieldIndex) returns FieldIndex
	var size = 1 as FieldIndex
	let slots = surfaceNodeSlotCount(surfaceNodeAt(surface, at: at))
	for _ in 0 upto slots 'eachSlot'
		size = size + surfaceSubtreeSize(surface, at: at + size)
	end 'eachSlot'
	return size
end 'surfaceSubtreeSize'

// Fold a declared surface into a running hash — every node plus the node COUNT, so a tree that gained a
// slot cannot collide with the one it grew from (`mixBytes`' trailing length tag, for its reason). The
// surface is the one declaration fact `mixType` structurally cannot carry: `__ManagedMemory` and `ByteArray`
// fold to the identical `genericInstance`, so an edit between the two spellings would move no memo key while
// changing which member roster every caller's calls reach.
export function mixSurface(state ContentHash, surface DeclaredSurface) returns ContentHash
	var h = state
	for node in surface 'eachNode'
		h = mix(h, value: node)
	end 'eachNode'
	return mix(h, value: surface.count())
end 'mixSurface'

// The node index tuple slot `slot` starts at — node 0's first child, advanced past each earlier sibling's
// whole subtree.
function surfaceSlotStart(surface DeclaredSurface, slot FieldIndex) returns FieldIndex
	var at = 1 as FieldIndex
	for _ in 0 upto slot 'skipEarlierSlots'
		at = at + surfaceSubtreeSize(surface, at: at)
	end 'skipEarlierSlots'
	return at
end 'surfaceSlotStart'

// ⭐ **THE THIRTEEN `__ManagedFile` METHODS — the surface BOTH references agree on line-for-line**
// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:1414-1440`, `maxon-selfhosted/.../LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1801-1842`),
// complete as of R4.2. Nine statics and four instance methods; a name outside this set is refused by name
// at its own span rather than mangled into a callee no file declares
// (`Parser.parseManagedFileStaticCall` / `Parser.dispatchManagedFileMethod`).
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `seek`.** v1 has an `lseek` primitive, but it belongs to a different v1
// abstraction and neither reference's `__ManagedFile` method list carries one — a fourteenth method here
// would be shv2 inventing surface the language does not have.
export let ManagedFileOpenReadMethod = b"openRead"
export let ManagedFileOpenWriteMethod = b"openWrite"

// ⭐ It is a distinct METHOD and NOT a distinct op: its only difference in either reference is a Unix 0755
// permission bit, and shv2's file IO is host-only Windows by the R4.1 ruling — so it lowers to exactly
// `openWrite`'s `osFileOpenWrite`. A POSIX lane splits them on the day it exists.
export let ManagedFileOpenWriteExecutableMethod = b"openWriteExecutable"
export let ManagedFileExistsMethod = b"exists"
export let ManagedFileDeleteMethod = b"delete"
export let ManagedFileRenameMethod = b"rename"
export let ManagedFileStatMethod = b"stat"
export let ManagedFileStatFieldMethod = b"statField"
export let ManagedFileStatFreeMethod = b"statFree"
export let ManagedFileCloseMethod = b"close"
export let ManagedFileSizeMethod = b"size"
export let ManagedFileReadMethod = b"read"
export let ManagedFileWriteMethod = b"write"

// ⭐ **THE PARAMETER NAMES A `__ManagedFile` ARGUMENT MISMATCH QUOTES BACK.** They are the reference's own
// (`2-Parser.cs:1414-1440`), and they are what `builtin-type-checking.error-managed-file-*` pins: the
// oracle reports `argument type mismatch for 'path'`, so the name is a fact about the SIGNATURE, not a
// word this compiler picks.
// ⚠ Shared with `__ManagedDirectory` (R4.3): both references name the path parameter of `openSearch`,
// `exists` and `create` `path` too, so this is one word for one fact rather than a per-family copy.
export let ManagedFilePathParamName = b"path"
export let ManagedFileOldPathParamName = b"oldPath"
export let ManagedFileNewPathParamName = b"newPath"
export let ManagedFileManagedParamName = b"managed"
export let ManagedFileSizeParamName = b"size"
export let ManagedFileBufferParamName = b"buffer"
export let ManagedFileIndexParamName = b"index"

// ⭐ **THE `__ManagedMemory` OBJECT SURFACE, COMPLETE AS OF R4.4** — the twelve members the corpus reaches,
// each of them the reference's own name (v1 `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1618-1660`, bootstrap
// `2-Parser.cs:1470-1512`). SIX of them are spelled IDENTICALLY to an `Array` method and dispatch to the
// identical runtime entry (`capacity`, `get`, `set`, `append`, `slice`, `clear`); `length` shares the ENTRY
// with `Array.count` under a DIFFERENT spelling, which is why `mm.count()` is refused (D11b's
// `error.buffer-has-no-count`) — a shared emission is not a shared name; four are the buffer's own
// (`setLength`, `setByte`, `grow`, `byteAt`); the twelfth is the `create` static.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE SET IS NOT STATED HERE — IT IS `Parser.bufferSurfaceMemberNames`, WHICH IS ALSO WHAT THE REFUSAL
// PRINTS (D11b).** This block declares the SPELLINGS; the roster declares MEMBERSHIP, and the paragraph
// above is a reader's summary of it rather than a second authority. It had been exactly such a second
// authority, and it was wrong TWICE — it said SIX and listed seven, then D11b said SEVEN and kept the same
// seven, which reads `length` as an `Array` spelling and so contradicts the case above — while the dispatch
// it described accepted twelve `Array` names the message denied.
//
// ⚠ **`elementSize`, `remove`, `swap`, `shiftLeft`/`shiftRight`, `toCString`, `makeCharFromBytes`,
// `fromCString` and `createCursor` are DELIBERATELY ABSENT** — the reference's member table is 19 deep, this
// is the other 8 of it plus the `fromCString` static. None has a caller shv2 can reach: `elementSize` has
// twelve (`specs/ranged-int-bit-packing.md`, whose subject is ranged-int bit packing shv2 does not have) and
// `remove`/`swap`/`shiftRight`/`elementSize`/`toCString`/`makeCharFromBytes` have `stdlib/Array.maxon`, which
// no whitelisted module imports and which shv2 cannot compile at all; the four cstring/cursor members need a
// `cstring` type shv2 has no producer for. A member with no reachable caller is a mechanism handed a consumer
// that cannot exercise it. `Parser.parseManagedMemoryStaticCall` (the statics) and `Parser.dispatchArrayMethod`
// (the members) name them in their refusals so the gap is a diagnostic rather than a silence.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO REFUSALS EACH MISFILE ONE NAME, and the reference's own comment is where it came from:**
// `createCursor` is registered `["self"], [mm]` — an INSTANCE method — under a `// static methods` heading
// (`2-Parser.cs:1517-1523`), so the static refusal calls it a static and the member refusal calls
// `fromCString` (genuinely `isStatic: true`) a member. Neither is a false statement about BUILDABILITY, which
// is what the sentences are for; correcting the category costs 34 committed goldens, since the `are not built`
// clause is a literal in the message rather than rendered from a list the way the roster now is.
export let ManagedMemoryCreateMethod = b"create"
export let ManagedMemorySetLengthMethod = b"setLength"
export let ManagedMemorySetByteMethod = b"setByte"
export let ManagedMemoryLengthMethod = b"length"
export let ManagedMemoryGrowMethod = b"grow"
export let ManagedMemoryByteAtMethod = b"byteAt"

// ⭐ **THE `Array` SPELLINGS A SOURCE-METHOD MAP HAS TO NAME (D12, completed by D11c).** Every one of these
// was a PARSER constant, file-private in `Parser.maxon`, and each moved here the moment a runtime family's
// source-method map had to answer with it: a map cannot name a constant its file cannot see, and the
// alternative — a second `b"set"` beside the first — is exactly the drift these blocks exist to prevent.
//
// `set` and `append` came over at D12 for `ArrayRuntime.managedMemorySourceMethodName` (the buffer's two
// shared spellings). **D11c brought the other six**, because `ArrayRuntime.arrayAccessorSourceMethodName`
// now answers for all nine throwing `Array` accessors rather than for the buffer subset alone — see that
// map for why the array family stopped being the one family without a spelling.
//
// ⚠ None carries a family prefix, and that is the fact rather than an omission: ONE spelling serves
// `String.append`, `Array.append`/`Array.set` and the buffer's `__arr_mem_append`/`__arr_mem_set` alike, and
// the parser dispatches all of them on this one byte string. `RemoveMethod` and `SliceMethod` are unprefixed
// for the same reason — the buffer and the `Array` both spell `slice`.
export let ArraySetMethod = b"set"
export let AppendMethod = b"append"
export let ArrayGetMethod = b"get"
export let ArrayFirstMethod = b"first"
export let ArrayLastMethod = b"last"
export let ArrayPopMethod = b"pop"
export let RemoveMethod = b"remove"
export let SliceMethod = b"slice"

// Its parameter names, the reference's own (`2-Parser.cs:1470-1511`) for the same reason the file's are.
// ⚠ **`endIndex` IS v1's SPELLING AND THE BOOTSTRAP'S IS `end`** (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1657` vs
// `2-Parser.cs:1509`), and `specs/managed-memory-builtin.md:slice` writes `endIndex:` — that spec is
// `status: selfhosted`, so v1's is the one the corpus pins, and it is also `Array.slice`'s own label
// (`EndIndexArgLabel`), which the SAME dispatch arm serves. Measured: the bootstrap answers
// `E3003 unknown parameter name: 'endIndex'` for it.
export let ManagedMemoryCountParamName = b"count"
export let ManagedMemoryElementSizeParamName = b"elementSize"
export let ManagedMemoryNewLengthParamName = b"newLength"
export let ManagedMemoryNewCapacityParamName = b"newCapacity"
export let ManagedMemoryOffsetParamName = b"offset"
export let ManagedMemoryIndexParamName = b"index"
export let ManagedMemoryOtherParamName = b"other"
export let ManagedMemoryValueParamName = b"value"

// ⭐ **THE MACHINE-WORD ELEMENT WIDTH A `__ManagedMemory.create(count, 8)` NAMES (R4.4 review)** — the
// second and last of the two widths shv2 can give a buffer an element TYPE for, `ByteStringElementSize`
// being the first. It is `LayoutDescriptor.MachineWordBytes` in the type a PARSED source constant arrives
// as, spelled here for the reason `ManagedArrayElementBytes` is spelled in this file: a `ByteOffset` and a
// `ParsedInt` are distinct types, and the comparison happens on the parsed side.
// ⇒ Read by `managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize` (which width maps to which instance) and by
// `managedMemoryElementSizeIsTypeable` (which widths are admitted at all), so those two answer about ONE
// set of widths rather than two lists that can drift.
let ManagedMemoryWordElementSize = 8 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE ERROR A `__ManagedMemory` MEMBER THROWS (R4.2)** — `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:151-160`, and NOT
// `ArrayError`: `invalidLength` and `invalidByteRange` name conditions `ArrayError` has no case for, so a
// handler that binds the error would be shown cases the operation cannot report. The eight cases are the
// reference's, in the reference's ORDER, because the ordinal IS the error flag the runtime returns — the same
// wire format `__ManagedFileError`'s order is.
//
// ⚠ R4.2 delivers three of the eight-case surface's members (`create`/`setLength`/`setByte`), so five of
// these cases are unreachable TODAY. They are declared anyway rather than added one per rung: the ordinals
// are positional, so a later rung that appends `sliceOutOfBounds` between two of them would silently reroute
// every handler already written against the others.
export let ManagedMemoryErrorTypeName = b"__ManagedMemoryError"

// ⭐⭐ **`ArrayError` — WHAT EVERY THROWING `Array` ACCESSOR THROWS, AND WHY IT WEARS A RESERVED NAME (R4.4).**
// `stdlib/Array.maxon:6-9` declares it with exactly two cases, and `specs-shv2/array-slots.md` discriminates
// both (`emptySlot` for an index INSIDE `[0, count())` whose slot was never filled, `indexOutOfBounds` for one
// that is not) — so the enum has to be REGISTERED, or `match e` over an `arr.get` failure reports a missing
// FEATURE for a program whose feature is present. That is the same gap R4.1 closed for `__ManagedFileError` and
// R4.2's review closed for `__ManagedMemoryError`; this is the third and last family.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT IS THE BARE NAME, NOT A RESERVED `__ArrayError`, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** The
// two sibling builtin error enums are `__`-prefixed (`StringIndexTypeName` gives the argument: a user
// declaration of the same name would land in the same map bucket and the later write would win). This one
// cannot be, because USER SOURCE ALREADY NAMES IT: four committed cases declare `function … throws ArrayError`
// and PROPAGATE an array accessor's failure through it (`array-slice-managed-elements.md` ×3,
// `borrow-liveness.md`). Under `__ArrayError` every one of them became
// `E3059: try propagates '__ArrayError' but enclosing function throws 'ArrayError'` — one error type under two
// spellings, which is the very confusion the prefix exists to prevent.
// ⇒ So `ArrayError` is RESERVED PROGRAM-WIDE here, exactly as whitelisting a stdlib module reserves its type
// names (`StdlibLoader`'s collision rule) and as `CharSet` already is — and for the same reason: shv2
// synthesizes what `stdlib/Array.maxon` declares, so it owes that file's exported names.
export let ArrayErrorTypeName = b"ArrayError"

// ⭐ **`ArrayError`'s TWO CASES ARE `__ManagedMemoryError`'s FIRST TWO, IN THE SAME ORDER, AND THAT IS THE
// REFERENCE'S OWN ARRANGEMENT** (`stdlib/Array.maxon:6-9` against `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:151-153`). So ONE pair
// of name constants serves both enums: writing them twice would let the two orders drift apart while each file
// still read "correct", and the ordinal IS the error flag a throwing runtime returns in both families.
export let ManagedMemoryIndexOutOfBoundsCaseName = b"indexOutOfBounds"
export let ManagedMemoryEmptySlotCaseName = b"emptySlot"
export let ManagedMemorySliceOutOfBoundsCaseName = b"sliceOutOfBounds"
export let ManagedMemoryInvalidLengthCaseName = b"invalidLength"
export let ManagedMemoryInvalidCapacityCaseName = b"invalidCapacity"
export let ManagedMemoryShiftOutOfBoundsCaseName = b"shiftOutOfBounds"
export let ManagedMemoryInvalidByteRangeCaseName = b"invalidByteRange"
export let ManagedMemoryInvalidAllocationCaseName = b"invalidAllocation"

// `String.init(managed ByteMemory)` — `stdlib/String.maxon:114`, the corpus's way back from a
// `__ManagedMemory` to a `String`. Its parameter name is the reference's.
export let StringInitMethod = b"init"
export let StringInitManagedParamName = b"managed"

// ⭐ **THE TWO THROWING `String` SEARCHES (P1.8 slice B2), MOVED OUT OF `Parser.maxon` AT D12.** They are the
